- Integrated Carer Support Service - Carer Gateway Service Providers - CGSPs - Addendum 5.PDF.txt', 'extracted_text': ' \n \n \n \n \n Amendment to the Integrated Carer Support Service: Carer Gateway Service Providers \n(CGSPs) Grant Opportunity \nGO8455 - Addendum Number 5 \n \nDate Issued: 18 August 2026  \nThis addendum is issued as an amendment to grant opportunity GO8554. The amendment is as specified hereunder. \nChanges to the Frequently Asked Questions:   \nDelete \nInsert \nNA \nInsert Section 1.49.  \n \n\n \nAddendum 5 ? GO8455 \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n2 \n \nDelete \nInsert \nNA \nInsert Section 3.24  \n \n \n \n\n'}, {'file_name': 'GO8455 - Integrated Carer Support Service - Carer Gateway Service Providers - CGSPs - Addendum 5.txt', 'extracted_text': ' \n \n \n \n \n Amendment to the Integrated Carer Support Service: Carer Gateway Service Providers \n(CGSPs) Grant Opportunity \nGO8455 - Addendum Number 5 \n \nDate Issued: 18 August 2026  \nThis addendum is issued as an amendment to grant opportunity GO8554. The amendment is as specified hereunder. \nChanges to the Frequently Asked Questions:   \nDelete \nInsert \nNA \nInsert Section 1.49.  \n \n\n \nAddendum 5 ? GO8455 \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n2 \n \nDelete \nInsert \nNA \nInsert Section 3.24  \n \n \n \n\n'}, {'file_name': 'GO8455 - Integrated Carer Support Service - Carer Gateway Service Providers - CGSPs - Addendum 6.PDF.txt', 'extracted_text': ' \n \n \n \nAmendment to the Integrated Carer Support Service: Carer Gateway Service Providers \n(CGSPs) Grant Opportunity \nGO8455 - Addendum Number 6 \n \nDate Issued: 25 August 2026  \nThis addendum is issued as an amendment to grant opportunity GO8455. The amendment is as specified hereunder. \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n\n \nAddendum 6 ? GO8455 \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n2 \n \n \n \nChanges to the Frequently Asked Questions (FAQs) \nDelete \nInsert \nNA \n1.50 Can the department clarify the required operating \nhours for CGSP service delivery under the Grant \nOpportunity? \nFor the grant period from 1 July 2027 to 30 June 2032, Carer \nGateway Service Providers must be available to the public during \nbusiness operating hours from Monday to Friday, 8am to 8pm. \nPlease refer to section 7.16 of the Carer Gateway Service Providers \nOperating Manual for more information. \n1.51 Does the model allow facilitation of warm \nreferrals from priority partner organisations, e.g. warm \nreferral from ACCHO organisations, rather than asking \nthem to refer carers to the national number? \nService providers are expected to proactively engage with a broad \nrange of organisations, including ACCHO organisations, to support \ntwo-way referral and navigation pathways that improve access to \nappropriate supports for carers. Warm referral pathways that do not \ninvolve referring the carers back out to the national 1800 phone \nnumber are encouraged. Service providers are expected to link \ncarers to available resources, including internal Carer Gateway \nservices and relevant external services, using warm referral \napproaches where appropriate to actively support engagement. \nPlease refer to sections 9 and 11 of the Carer Gateway Service \nProviders Operating Manual for more information. \n \n\n \nAddendum 6 ? GO8455 \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n3 \n \n \nDelete \nInsert \nNA \n3.25 Are references or referee reports required as part \nof an application? \nApplicants are not required to provide referee details or upload \nreferences as part of the application pack. For further information on \nthe assessment and selection process, please refer to Section 8: The \ngrant selection process in the Grant Opportunity Guidelines. \n \n \n\n'}, {'file_name': 'GO8455 - Integrated Carer Support Service - Carer Gateway Service Providers - CGSPs - Addendum 6.txt', 'extracted_text': ' \n \n \n \nAmendment to the Integrated Carer Support Service: Carer Gateway Service Providers \n(CGSPs) Grant Opportunity \nGO8455 - Addendum Number 6 \n \nDate Issued: 25 August 2026  \nThis addendum is issued as an amendment to grant opportunity GO8455. The amendment is as specified hereunder. \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n\n \nAddendum 6 ? GO8455 \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n2 \n \n \n \nChanges to the Frequently Asked Questions (FAQs) \nDelete \nInsert \nNA \n1.50 Can the department clarify the required operating \nhours for CGSP service delivery under the Grant \nOpportunity? \nFor the grant period from 1 July 2027 to 30 June 2032, Carer \nGateway Service Providers must be available to the public during \nbusiness operating hours from Monday to Friday, 8am to 8pm. \nPlease refer to section 7.16 of the Carer Gateway Service Providers \nOperating Manual for more information. \n1.51 Does the model allow facilitation of warm \nreferrals from priority partner organisations, e.g. warm \nreferral from ACCHO organisations, rather than asking \nthem to refer carers to the national number? \nService providers are expected to proactively engage with a broad \nrange of organisations, including ACCHO organisations, to support \ntwo-way referral and navigation pathways that improve access to \nappropriate supports for carers. Warm referral pathways that do not \ninvolve referring the carers back out to the national 1800 phone \nnumber are encouraged. Service providers are expected to link \ncarers to available resources, including internal Carer Gateway \nservices and relevant external services, using warm referral \napproaches where appropriate to actively support engagement. \nPlease refer to sections 9 and 11 of the Carer Gateway Service \nProviders Operating Manual for more information. \n \n\n \nAddendum 6 ? GO8455 \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n3 \n \n \nDelete \nInsert \nNA \n3.25 Are references or referee reports required as part \nof an application? \nApplicants are not required to provide referee details or upload \nreferences as part of the application pack. For further information on \nthe assessment and selection process, please refer to Section 8: The \ngrant selection process in the Grant Opportunity Guidelines. \n \n \n\n'}, {'file_name': 'GO8455 - Integrated Carer Support Service - Carer Gateway Service Providers - CGSPs - Addendum 7.PDF.txt', 'extracted_text': ' \n \n \n \n \n Amendment to the Integrated Carer Support Service: Carer Gateway Service Providers \n(CGSPs) Grant Opportunity ? GO8455 \nAddendum Number 7 \nDate Issued: 3 September 2026 \nThis addendum is issued as an amendment to grant opportunity GO8538. The amendment is as specified hereunder. \nChanges to the Frequently Asked Questions:  \nDelete \nInsert \nNA \nInsert the below wording into section 1.26  \n \n\n \nAddendum 7 ? GO8455 \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n2 \n \n \nDelete \nInsert \nNA  \nInsert Section 1.52  \n \nNA \nInsert Section 1.53 \n \n\n \nAddendum 7 ? GO8455 \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n3 \n \n \nDelete \nInsert \nNA  \nInsert Section 3.26  \n \n\n \nAddendum 7 ? GO8455 \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n4 \n \n \nDelete \nInsert \nNA \nInsert Section 3.27  \n \n \n\n'}, {'file_name': 'GO8455 - Integrated Carer Support Service - Carer Gateway Service Providers - CGSPs - Addendum 7.txt', 'extracted_text': ' \n \n \n \n \n Amendment to the Integrated Carer Support Service: Carer Gateway Service Providers \n(CGSPs) Grant Opportunity ? GO8455 \nAddendum Number 7 \nDate Issued: 3 September 2026 \nThis addendum is issued as an amendment to grant opportunity GO8538. The amendment is as specified hereunder. \nChanges to the Frequently Asked Questions:  \nDelete \nInsert \nNA \nInsert the below wording into section 1.26  \n \n\n \nAddendum 7 ? GO8455 \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n2 \n \n \nDelete \nInsert \nNA  \nInsert Section 1.52  \n \nNA \nInsert Section 1.53 \n \n\n \nAddendum 7 ? GO8455 \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n3 \n \n \nDelete \nInsert \nNA  \nInsert Section 3.26  \n \n\n \nAddendum 7 ? GO8455 \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n4 \n \n \nDelete \nInsert \nNA \nInsert Section 3.27  \n \n \n\n'}, {'file_name': 'GO8455 - Integrated Carer Support Service - Carer Gateway Service Providers - CGSPs - Addendum 8.PDF.txt', 'extracted_text': ' \n \n \n \n \nAmendment to the Integrated Carer Support Service: Carer Gateway Service Providers \n(CGSPs) Grant Opportunity ? GO8455 \nAddendum Number 8 \nDate Issued: 8 September 2026 \nThis addendum is issued as an amendment to grant opportunity GO8455. The amendment is as specified hereunder. \nChanges to the Frequently Asked Questions:  \n\n \nAddendum 8 ? GO8455 \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n2 \n \n \nDelete \nInsert \nNA  \nInsert Section 1.54  \n \n \n \n\n \nAddendum 8 ? GO8455 \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n3 \n \n \nDelete \nInsert \nNA  \nInsert Section 3.28 \n \nNA \nInsert Section 3.29 \n \n \n\n \nAddendum 8 ? GO8455 \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n4 \n \n \nDelete \nInsert \nNA \nInsert Section 3.30  \n \n \n\n'}, {'file_name': 'GO8455 - Integrated Carer Support Service - Carer Gateway Service Providers - CGSPs - Addendum 8.txt', 'extracted_text': ' \n \n \n \n \nAmendment to the Integrated Carer Support Service: Carer Gateway Service Providers \n(CGSPs) Grant Opportunity ? GO8455 \nAddendum Number 8 \nDate Issued: 8 September 2026 \nThis addendum is issued as an amendment to grant opportunity GO8455. The amendment is as specified hereunder. \nChanges to the Frequently Asked Questions:  \n\n \nAddendum 8 ? GO8455 \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n2 \n \n \nDelete \nInsert \nNA  \nInsert Section 1.54  \n \n \n \n\n \nAddendum 8 ? GO8455 \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n3 \n \n \nDelete \nInsert \nNA  \nInsert Section 3.28 \n \nNA \nInsert Section 3.29 \n \n \n\n \nAddendum 8 ? GO8455 \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n4 \n \n \nDelete \nInsert \nNA \nInsert Section 3.30  \n \n \n\n'}, {'file_name': 'GO8455 - Integrated Carer Support Service - Carer Gateway Service Providers - CGSPs - Application Form - URL Only.PDF.txt', 'extracted_text': ' \nOnline Application Form ? GO8455 \n \n1 \n \n \n \nIntegrated Carer Support Service:  \nCarer Gateway Service Providers (CGSPs)Online \nApplication Form  \nGO8455 \n \nTo start a new application for this grant opportunity, select the Start Application button \nbelow. \n \n \n? You must read through all grant opportunity documents available on GrantConnect before \napplying.  \n? Your application form will be accessible for 60 days from the last date accessed or the \nclose date/time of the round, whichever date is earliest. After the 60-day period has \npassed the form is no longer available for amendments or submission (data cannot be \nretrieved once this period has lapsed). You will need to submit a new application if the \ngrant opportunity is still open. \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n\n'}, {'file_name': 'GO8455 - Integrated Carer Support Service - Carer Gateway Service Providers - CGSPs - Application Form - URL Only.txt', 'extracted_text': ' \nOnline Application Form ? GO8455 \n \n1 \n \n \n \nIntegrated Carer Support Service:  \nCarer Gateway Service Providers (CGSPs)Online \nApplication Form  \nGO8455 \n \nTo start a new application for this grant opportunity, select the Start Application button \nbelow. \n \n \n? You must read through all grant opportunity documents available on GrantConnect before \napplying.  \n? Your application form will be accessible for 60 days from the last date accessed or the \nclose date/time of the round, whichever date is earliest. After the 60-day period has \npassed the form is no longer available for amendments or submission (data cannot be \nretrieved once this period has lapsed). You will need to submit a new application if the \ngrant opportunity is still open. \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n\n'}, {'file_name': 'GO8455 - Integrated Carer Support Service - Carer Gateway Service Providers - CGSPs - Attachment Pack.XLSX.txt', 'extracted_text': '## Sheet: Buisness Model\nBusiness Model\t\t\t\t\nCarer Gateway Service Providers (CGSPs) - GO8455\t\t\t\t\nGuidance: Complete the name and ABN of your organisation along with the details of the service area you are applying for. All content must relate to the grant activity to be funded under this application process if successful. Please create a new sheet for every service area applied for and identify where service delivery will benefit from economy of scale. Complete all fields in red in the table to describe your plan for maintaining your business model in each service area you are applying for, as it relates to Carer Gateway service delivery, governance, leadership and management, and working in partnership with the community. Each field has a character limit of 900 characters (approximately 150 words). Do not add more rows to the table. Refer to the grant opportunity guidelines and the Carer Gateway Operating Manual for further guidance.\t\t\t\t\n<Insert Organisation name & ABN here>\t\t\t<Specify the service area this template relates to>\t\n\t\t\t<if applying for more than one service area, specify the other areas you are applying for>\t\nCARER GATEWAY SERVICE DELIVERY\t\t\t\t\nConsider here: How you will address identified priority carer needs in your region/communities including priority cohorts identified in the National Carer Strategy.\t\t\t\t\nObjective(s)\nWhat are you trying to achieve (outcome focused)? \tStrategy(ies)/Activity(ies)\nHow will you do this? \tPerformance Indicator(s)\nHow will you measure performance? \tTarget(s)\nWhat are your targets?\tTimeframe(s)\nWhen will this be delivered each year? \nCarer support planning\nAn intake and needs assessment process for the carer consistent with the Carer Support Planning framework in order to determine the services which best suit the needs of individual carers. \t<insert>\t<insert>\t<insert>\t<insert>\nIn person peer support \nFree facilitated peer support forums and activities specifically designed to assist carers to improve their social connectedness, help them connect with people in similar circumstnaces in a safe environment and learn from peers to positively support carers\' emotional wellbeing. \t<insert>\t<insert>\t<insert>\t<insert>\nIn-person counselling \nA free counselling service for carers who are experiencing difficulties with anxiety, stress, depression and low mood as a result of their caring role. The service will assist carers to manage their own health in order to remain effective in their caring role and avoid crisis events through in-person counselling sessions with a professional counsellor. \t<insert>\t<insert>\t<insert>\t<insert>\nIn-person carer coaching\nA free psychoeducational service specifically desgined to assist carers to acquire the skills and resilience needed in their caring role. Service providers may deliver the \'in-person\' coaching face-to-face, via phone, or via digital channels such as video-calling. \t<insert>\t<insert>\t<insert>\t<insert>\nTailored Supports \nDetermined by the agreed carer needs assessments to support carers in their caring role by providing a package of tailored supports and services that are inclusive, flexible, timely and based on the carer\'s level of need, where they live and that are culturally safe. The service is delivered in 2 formats - through the provision of (a) one-off practical supports or (b) a tailored support package. \t<insert>\t<insert>\t<insert>\t<insert>\nCoordinated access to emergency respite care\nCoordinates access and referrals to emergency respite supports for carers who are experiencing an urgent, unplanned and imminent event that temporarily restricts their ability to continue caring when no other services are available. CGSPs will assist carers to access local supports and services and, if they see a need, they will work with local providers to undertake functions such as managing respite bookings.\t<insert>\t<insert>\t<insert>\t<insert>\nVocational Pathways\nProvision of vocational support includes in-house or brokered skills training (resume writing, interview skills, etc); and/or provides carers with access to Registered Training Organisations (RTO) accredited training (e.g. Cert II to IV) and/or provides carers with case management to support successful completion of training ? including goals, identified needs to support attendance at training, etc. \t<insert>\t<insert>\t<insert>\t<insert>\nGOVERNANCE, LEADERSHIP AND MANAGEMENT\t\t\t\t\nConsider here: Senior Management and Board expertise; workforce development, strategic planning, accreditation (where relevant), quality assurance and governance; financial, IT, communication, reporting and client information systems; infrastructure; business risks and mitigation; carer needs and service planning. \t\t\t\t\nObjective(s)\nWhat are you trying to achieve (outcome focused)? \tStrategy(ies)/Activity(ies)\nHow will you do this? \tPerformance Indicator(s)\nHow will you measure performance? \tTarget(s)\nWhat are your targets?\tTimeframe(s)\nWhen will this be delivered? \nGovernance\nEnter details here regarding the governance arrangements which will be in place to monitor and maintain delivery of grant requirements.\t<insert>\t<insert>\t<insert>\t<insert>\nWorkforce Planning\nEnter details here regarding the workforce that will be  in place to deliver CGSP activities. This should include the planned approach to maintaining appropriate staffing and corporate knowledge.\t<insert>\t<insert>\t<insert>\t<insert>\nInfrastructure\nEnter details here regarding the infrastructure arrangements (both ICT and non-ICT related) which will be in place and how they will be maintained. \nRefer to the CGSPs Operating Manual for a list of the infrastructure requirements for CGSPs. \t<insert>\t<insert>\t<insert>\t<insert>\nOperating guidelines and policies \nEnter details here regarding how the policies at section 4.5 of the grant opportunity guidlines and section 7.2 of the operating manual will be maintained to support high quality service provision throughout the life of the grant agreement.\t<insert>\t<insert>\t<insert>\t<insert>\nContinuous Improvement\nEnter details here regarding how you will invest in innovation and continuous improvement activities to improve carer outcomes and contribute to the objectives of the National Carer Strategy and associated Actions.\t<insert>\t<insert>\t<insert>\t<insert>\nWORKING IN PARTNERSHIP WITH THE COMMUNITY\t\t\t\t\nConsider here: Mechanisms to consult with community members on planning, design and implementation of services; community and client feedback and complaint processes; information dissemination to community around carer issues and service performance.\t\t\t\t\nObjective(s)\nWhat are you trying to achieve (outcome focused)? \tStrategy(ies)/Activity(ies)\nHow will you do this? \tPerformance Indicator(s)\nHow will you measure performance? \tTarget(s)\nWhat are your targets?\tTimeframe(s)\nWhen will this be delivered? \nService Area Planning and Outreach\nService providers must, on an annual basis, undertake a service area mapping exercise of their service area/s. The purpose of this mapping is to identify organisations delivering services which may be of benefit to carers, and to develop relationships with these organisations for the purpose of referring carers to these services. It is expected that service providers will need to utilise a wide range of existing services to meet the needs of carers.\t<insert>\t<insert>\t<insert>\t<insert>\nPromotion of Additional Carer Services\nService providers are responsible for undertaking promotional and profile-raising activities, including outreach activities to targeted groups such as young carers. This should include outreach activities within local communities and with local service providers. \nOutreach activities should also include promotion of online Carer Gateway services such as the\nSelf- guided Carer Coaching service, online Carer Skills Courses and the online Community Forum.\t<insert>\t<insert>\t<insert>\t<insert>\nEstablishment Plan\t\t\t\t\nCarer Gateway Service Providers (CGSPs) - GO8455\t\t\t\t\n<Insert Organisation name & ABN here>\t\t\t<Insert the name of your grant activity here>\t\nGuidance: Complete the name and ABN of your organisation and identify the service area this plan relates to. Please create a new sheet for every service area applied for and identify where service delivery will benefit from economy of scale. Complete all fields in red in the table to describe your plan for establishing your business model in each new service area you are applying for, as it relates to governance, workforce planning, infrastructure, operating guidelines and policies, and transition planning. Outline how you will ensure full operational readiness by 1 July 2027, assuming an approximately three-month establishment period. Each field has a character limit of 900 characters (approximately 150 words). Do not add more rows to the table. Refer to the grant opportunity guidelines and the Carer Gateway Operating Manual for further guidance.\t\t\t\t\nObjective(s)\nWhat are you trying to achieve (outcome focused)? \tStrategy(ies)/Activity(ies)\nHow will you do this? \tPerformance Indicator(s)\nHow will you measure performance? \tTarget(s)\nWhat are your targets?\tTimeframe(s)\nWhen will this be delivered? \nGovernance\nEnter details here regarding the governance arrangements which will be put in place to monitor progress toward delivery of the establishment plan.\t<insert>\t<insert>\t<insert>\t<insert>\nWorkforce Planning\nEnter details here regarding the workforce that will be put in place to deliver CGSP activities. This should include the planned approach to on boarding and training staff.\t<insert>\t<insert>\t<insert>\t<insert>\nInfrastructure\nEnter details here regarding the infrastructure arrangements (both ICT and non-ICT related) which will be put in place and how they will be established. \nRefer to section XX of the CGSPs Operating Manual for a list of the infrastructure requirements for CGSPs. \t<insert>\t<insert>\t<insert>\t<insert>\nOperating guidelines and policies \nEnter details here regarding how the policies at section 4.5 of the grant opportunity guidlines and section 7.2 of the operating manual will be developed to support high quality service provision within the first 6 months of operating.\t<insert>\t<insert>\t<insert>\t<insert>\nTransition planning\nEnter details here regarding how you will work with the incumbent CGSP in the region to transition carers information and service delivery whilst ensuring continuity of service.\t<insert>\t<insert>\t<insert>\t<insert>\n\n## Sheet: Indicative Activity Budget\nIndicative Activity Budget\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\nCarer Gateway Service Providers (CGSPs) - GO8455\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\n<Enter Organisation Name & ABN here>\t\t<Enter the name of the proposed service area here>\t\t\t\t\t\t\nGuidance: Include a detailed budget per region by providing clear costings (include the source/basis of the estimates) for essential budget items outlined below. Please create a new sheet for every service region applied for and clearly indicate where benefits will result from economies of scale. Each field has a character limit of 900 characters (approximately 150 words). Do not add more rows to the table. Please ensure all figures are GST Exclusive. \t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\nOperational Expenses\t\tNotes/basis of estimate/breakdown of items \n(e.g. 2 full time staff members, Software updates for system, etc.)\t2027/28\n$ GST Excl.\t2028/29\n$ GST Excl.\t2029/30\n$ GST Excl.\t2030/31\n$ GST Excl.\t2031/32\n$ GST Excl.\tTOTAL\n$ GST Excl.\nDirect FTE\tSalaries / Wages\t<insert>\t<insert>\t<insert>\t<insert>\t<insert>\t<insert>\t0\n\tOn-Costs\t<insert>\t<insert>\t<insert>\t<insert>\t<insert>\t<insert>\t0\n\tRegional and remote costs\t<insert>\t<insert>\t<insert>\t<insert>\t<insert>\t<insert>\t0\n\tOther\t<insert>\t<insert>\t<insert>\t<insert>\t<insert>\t<insert>\t0\nIndirect FTE\tSalaries / Wages\t<insert>\t<insert>\t<insert>\t<insert>\t<insert>\t<insert>\t0\n\tOn-Costs\t<insert>\t<insert>\t<insert>\t<insert>\t<insert>\t<insert>\t0\n\tRegional and remote costs\t<insert>\t<insert>\t<insert>\t<insert>\t<insert>\t<insert>\t0\n\tOther\t<insert>\t<insert>\t<insert>\t<insert>\t<insert>\t<insert>\t0\nService Delivery\tClient Support Services \t<insert>\t<insert>\t<insert>\t<insert>\t<insert>\t<insert>\t0\n\tClient Support consumables\t<insert>\t<insert>\t<insert>\t<insert>\t<insert>\t<insert>\t0\n\tTraining & Development (excl Board / Governance)\t<insert>\t<insert>\t<insert>\t<insert>\t<insert>\t<insert>\t0\n\tConsultants/Sub-Contractors\t<insert>\t<insert>\t<insert>\t<insert>\t<insert>\t<insert>\t\nInfrastructure\tRent\t<insert>\t<insert>\t<insert>\t<insert>\t<insert>\t<insert>\t0\n\tUtilities\t<insert>\t<insert>\t<insert>\t<insert>\t<insert>\t<insert>\t0\n\tCommunications & IT including software and hardware\t<insert>\t<insert>\t<insert>\t<insert>\t<insert>\t<insert>\t0\n\tInsurance (excl Motor Vehicle)\t<insert>\t<insert>\t<insert>\t<insert>\t<insert>\t<insert>\t0\n\tMotor Vehicle Expenses (incl Insurance and Repairs & Maintenance)\t<insert>\t<insert>\t<insert>\t<insert>\t<insert>\t<insert>\t0\nAdministrative\tHR / Corporate Services\t<insert>\t<insert>\t<insert>\t<insert>\t<insert>\t<insert>\t\n\tAccounting / Audit fees\t<insert>\t<insert>\t<insert>\t<insert>\t<insert>\t<insert>\t0\n\tAdvertising & Promotion\t<insert>\t<insert>\t<insert>\t<insert>\t<insert>\t<insert>\t0\n\tBoard / Governance (Travel, Accommodation & Training) \t<insert>\t<insert>\t<insert>\t<insert>\t<insert>\t<insert>\t0\n\tDepreciation\t<insert>\t<insert>\t<insert>\t<insert>\t<insert>\t<insert>\t0\n\tRepairs & Maintenance (excl Motor Vehicles)\t<insert>\t<insert>\t<insert>\t<insert>\t<insert>\t<insert>\t0\n\tTravel & Accommodation (excl Board / Governance)\t<insert>\t<insert>\t<insert>\t<insert>\t<insert>\t<insert>\t0\nOther Expenses (please include source, and list each separately - add rows)\t<insert>\t<insert>\t<insert>\t<insert>\t<insert>\t<insert>\t<insert>\t0\nTotal Operational Expenses\t\t\t0\t0\t0\t0\t0\t0\nEstablishment Budget\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\nCarer Gateway Service Providers (CGSPs) - GO8455\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\n<Enter Organisation Name & ABN here>\t\t<Enter the name of the proposed service area here>\t\t\t\t\t\t\nGuidance: Include a detailed establishment budget per region by providing clear costings (include the source/basis of the estimates) for essential budget items outlined below. Please create a new sheet for every service region applied for and clearly indicate where benefits will result from economies of scale. Each field has a character limit of 900 characters (approximately 150 words). Please ensure all figures are GST Exclusive. \t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\nEstablishment Expenses\t\tNotes/basis of estimate/breakdown of items \n(e.g. 2 full time staff members, Software updates for system, etc.)\t$ (GST Exclusive)\t\t\t\t\t\nSalaries / Wages\t\t<insert>\t<insert>\t\t\t\t\t\nOn-Costs\t\t<insert>\t<insert>\t\t\t\t\t\nRegional and remote costs\t\t<insert>\t<insert>\t\t\t\t\t\nOther\t\t<insert>\t<insert>\t\t\t\t\t\nSalaries / Wages\t\t<insert>\t<insert>\t\t\t\t\t\nOn-Costs\t\t<insert>\t<insert>\t\t\t\t\t\nRegional and remote costs\t\t<insert>\t<insert>\t\t\t\t\t\nOther\t\t<insert>\t<insert>\t\t\t\t\t\nClient Support Services \t\t<insert>\t<insert>\t\t\t\t\t\nClient Support consumables\t\t<insert>\t<insert>\t\t\t\t\t\nTraining & Development (excl Board / Governance)\t\t<insert>\t<insert>\t\t\t\t\t\nConsultants/Sub-Contractors\t\t<insert>\t<insert>\t\t\t\t\t\nRent\t\t<insert>\t<insert>\t\t\t\t\t\nUtilities\t\t<insert>\t<insert>\t\t\t\t\t\nCommunications & IT including software and hardware\t\t<insert>\t<insert>\t\t\t\t\t\nInsurance (excl Motor Vehicle)\t\t<insert>\t<insert>\t\t\t\t\t\nMotor Vehicle Expenses (incl Insurance and Repairs & Maintenance)\t\t<insert>\t<insert>\t\t\t\t\t\nHR / Corporate Services\t\t<insert>\t<insert>\t\t\t\t\t\nAccounting / Audit fees\t\t<insert>\t<insert>\t\t\t\t\t\nAdvertising & Promotion\t\t<insert>\t<insert>\t\t\t\t\t\nBoard / Governance (Travel, Accommodation & Training) \t\t<insert>\t<insert>\t\t\t\t\t\nDepreciation\t\t<insert>\t<insert>\t\t\t\t\t\nRepairs & Maintenance (excl Motor Vehicles)\t\t<insert>\t<insert>\t\t\t\t\t\nTravel & Accommodation (excl Board / Governance)\t\t<insert>\t<insert>\t\t\t\t\t\n<insert>\t\t<insert>\t<insert>\t\t\t\t\t\nTotal Establishment Expenses\t\t\t0\t\t\t\t\t\n\n## Sheet: Risk Management Plan\nRisk Management Plan\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\nCarer Gateway Service Providers (CGSPs) - GO8455\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\n<Insert Organisation name & ABN here>\t\t\t\t\t<Enter the name of your grant activity here>\t\t\t\t\nGuidance: Complete all fields in red in the table for risk associated with all regions you are applying for under this grant opportunity on a single page. Each field has a character limit of 900 characters (approximately 150 words). You must complete a minimum of three and no more than 5 risks. An example risk has been filled out in the first row. Please use the "Risk Information & Help" tab (next tab) for further guidance and examples.\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\nRisk Reference\tRisk\tRisk Identification (Source)\nWhat event(s) can happen and how it can happen?\tRisk Impact\nWhat are the effects if it does happen?\tRisk Controls\nWhat is currently in place to manage the risk?\tLikelihood\tConsequence\tCurrent risk rating\tAcceptable/ unacceptable?  \tRisk Treatments\nWhat (if any) additional work is planned or underway to manage the risk?\nExample\tPeople Management\t?\tInadequate mechanisms for staff accountability and performance management.\n?\tInadequate support for mental health issues resulting in increased stress levels and burnout among \nemployees. \n?\tInability to hire and maintain staff with the right skills. \t?\tHigher absenteeism\n?\tDecreased productivity\n?\tDiminished morale\n?\tDelays or inability to deliver Carer Gateway supports and services.\t?\tRegular team and 1:1 staff meetings to discuss performance and raise potential issues early.\n?\tEAP available to all staff.\n?\tLimits placed on staff working hours to prevent burnout. \n?\tPermanent staff have appropriate annual and personal leave entitlements.\n?\tCompetitive salaries and benefits packages attract and retain skilled staff.\tUnlikely\tMinor\tLow\tAcceptable \t?\tRisk has been rated acceptable, no further treatments required or planned.\n1\t<insert>\t<insert>\t<insert>\t<insert>\tChoose an item.\tChoose an item.\tChoose an item.\tChoose an item.\t<insert>\n2\t<insert>\t<insert>\t<insert>\t<insert>\tChoose an item.\tChoose an item.\tChoose an item.\tChoose an item.\t<insert>\n3\t<insert>\t<insert>\t<insert>\t<insert>\tChoose an item.\tChoose an item.\tChoose an item.\tChoose an item.\t<insert>\n4\t<insert>\t<insert>\t<insert>\t<insert>\tChoose an item.\tChoose an item.\tChoose an item.\tChoose an item.\t<insert>\n5\t<insert>\t<insert>\t<insert>\t<insert>\tChoose an item.\tChoose an item.\tChoose an item.\tChoose an item.\t<insert>\n\n## Sheet: Risk Information & Help\n\tRisk Information & Help\t\t\t\t\t\n\tRISKS\nA risk is defined as the effect of uncertainty on objectives1.  A risk is often expressed in terms of a combination of the consequences of an event (including changes in circumstances or knowledge) and the associated likelihood of occurrence.  \nWhere possible, try to combine similar risks to consolidate the number of potential risks.\t\t\t\t\t\n\tRISK IDENTIFICATION (SOURCE)\nThe purpose of risk identification is to find, recognise and describe risks that might prevent an organisation achieving its objectives.  When identifying risks the following questions should be considered;\n? What event(s) can happen that will have an adverse effect on the activity?\n? How can it happen?\n\t\t\t\t\t\n\tRISK IMPACT\nThe impact identifies the consequence of each risk (i.e. what are the effects to your organisation if it risk does happen?).\n\t\t\t\t\t\n\tRISK CONTROL\nA control is a current process, policy, device, practice or any other action designed to modify risk. Examples of controls include, checklists, meetings, procedures manual, contingency plans, audits and agreements in place.\n\t\t\t\t\t\n\tRISK TREATMENT\nA risk treatment is an additional activity being developed to manage and/or reduce the risk.  Examples of risk treatments include the creation of new guidelines, the introduction of a review process etc.  Once the proposed treatment has been implemented it becomes a control.\n\t\t\t\t\t\n\tEFFECTIVENESS OF RISK IDENTIFICATION\nAn adequate control implies that the risk is well managed and no further treatments are required.\nA marginally effective control implies that a treatment is not necessary however this may depend on the level of risk.\nAn inadequate control implies that treatments are necessary.\t\t\t\t\t\n\tLIKELIHOOD\nLikelihood is the chance that something might happen.  Likelihood can be defined, determined, or measured objectively or subjectively and can be expressed either qualitatively or quantitatively (using mathematics).\nRate the likelihood of the identified risk occurring with the controls in place.\nRatings are: Almost certain, Likely, Possible, Unlikely or Rare.\n\t\t\t\t\t\n\tCONSEQUENCE\nA consequence is the outcome of an event and has an effect on objectives. \nRate the consequence to the Project outcomes of the identified risk occurring with the controls in place.\nRatings are: Insignificant, Minor, Moderate, Major or Catastrophic.\n\t\t\t\t\t\n\t1Further information at: \tRisk Management - Principles and Guidelines 2018\t\t\t\t\n\tCURRENT RISK RATINGS\t\t\t\t\t\n\tLikelihood\tInsignificant Consequences\tMinor Consequences\tModerate Consequences\tMajor Consequences\tCatastrophic Consequences\n\tAlmost Certain\tLow\tMedium\tHigh\tExtreme\tExtreme\n\tLikely\tLow\tMedium\tHigh\tHigh\tExtreme\n\tPossible\tLow\tMedium\tMedium\tHigh\tExtreme\n\tUnlikely\tLow\tLow\tMedium\tMedium\tHigh\n\tRare\tLow\tLow\tMedium\tMedium\tMedium\n\tEXAMPLES OF RISK\t\t\t\t\t\n\tRisk\tLikelihood\nLow/Medium/High\n(comments)\tImpact\nLow/Medium/High\tMitigation Strategies\t\t\n\tDelays in receiving funding\te.g. Low\tHigh/Med/Low\tEnsure project deliverables are achieved in line with project plan and reported to the department in a timely fashion.\t\t\n\tDelays in recruitment of staff\tMay depend on nature, complexity and size of project.\tHigh/Med/Low\tRealistic understanding of what can be achieved within a limited timeframe and budget and awareness of what staff are likely to be available. \t\t\n\tBudget shortfalls\tProject has been appropriately scoped to cover all known contingencies. Project will be fully funded by Grant.\tHigh/Med/Low\tDevelop budgets to fully plan for known likelihoods and factor in relevant contingencies. Take a competitive approach to the market.\t\t\n\tBudget underspends \tWhere project is not rolled out in a timely fashion or has been inappropriately scoped in relation to expenditure. \tHigh/Med/Low\tEnsure a realistic project plan, timeline and budget are in place.\t\t\n\tOperational demands lead to delays to the timely delivery of project\tMay depend on scope of project; availability of appropriate staff; unforeseen circumstances; inadequate planning; unrealistic goals.\tHigh/Med/Low\tEnsure project manager/coordinator is fully capable and is working to a realistic project plan, timeline and budget can be enshrined in the Funding Agreement.\t\t\n\tCommunication with staff and/or target population\tInadequate or inappropriate methods of communication leads to breakdown in roll out of project.\tHigh/Med/Low\tCommunication with staff is incorporated into management plan along with a stakeholder engagement plan.\t\t\n\tTarget populations do not response to project\tLow uptake of project by target population.\tHigh/Med/Low\tThorough needs assessment and response is part of the project proposal and evidence of both need and adequacy of response is identified in project plan. Ongoing marketing and flexible project delivery.\t\t\n\n## Sheet: Data\n\tLikelihood\t\t\t\n\tChoose an item\tConsequence\tCurrent Risk Rating\tAcceptable/Unacceptable?\n\tRare\tInsignificant\tLow\tAcceptable \n\tUnlikely\tMinor\tMedium\tUnacceptable\n\tPossible\tModerate\tHigh\t\n\tLikely\tMajor\tExtreme\t\n\tAlmost Certain\tCatastrophic\t\t'}, {'file_name': 'GO8455 - Integrated Carer Support Service - Carer Gateway Service Providers - CGSPs - Carer Gateway Outcomes Framework - Version 2.PDF.txt', 'extracted_text': ' \n \nCarer Gateway ? Outcomes Framework \n\n \nCarer Gateway Outcomes Framework \n1 \nIntroduction \nCarer Gateway provides access to early intervention, preventative and skill building supports \nand services to improve wellbeing and long-term outcomes for the 3 million unpaid carers \nin Australia who are providing support for someone with a disability, a medical condition, \nmental illness or who is frail due to age. Unpaid carers play an integral role in the nation?s \nhealth and social care systems, offering critical, unrecognised contributions. \nThe Department of Health, Disability and Ageing (the department) works with Carer Gateway \nservice providers (CGSPs) by reviewing and refining the specific services and support \nofferings available for unpaid carers in a way that meets the carer?s specific needs and \nimproves the accessibility and inclusivity of Carer Gateway to support carers with their \ncaring role.  \nPurpose and structure of this document \nCaring is an important and natural part of our lives. The contribution of unpaid carers \nstrengthens the efficiency and effectiveness of our service systems and provides necessary \nadditional support when funded hours are unable to meet a person?s needs. The Carer \nGateway outcomes framework aims to measure the effectiveness, efficiency, equity and \nquality of the supports and services provided through the Integrated Carer Support Service \n(ICSS) Carer Gateway program for unpaid carers. \nThis document presents an outcomes framework for supports and services delivered under \nCarer Gateway by CGSPs. Specifically, the document provides a concept for:  \n1. A program logic to link Carer Gateway activities with outputs and short, medium and \nlong-term outcomes. A program logic model sets out the resources and activities \nthat comprise the program, and the changes that are expected to result from them.  \n2. An outcomes framework to identify the key indicators and measures that will be \nused to monitor and assess progress.  \nA program logic is a ?living document?, that is, it should be reviewed regularly to see if it is still \nan accurate representation of the program, meeting unpaid carers needs and ?fit for \npurpose? or if it needs to be adapted.  \n \n \n\n \nCarer Gateway Outcomes Framework \n2 \nProgram vision \nCaring can be stressful and can impact on the relationship between the carer and the \nperson they care for. It can also impact carers? economic and social wellbeing, including \ntheir ability to participate in everyday activities such as education and employment.  \nThe vision for the Carer Gateway program is: \nUnpaid carers are empowered with the support they need to participate fully in \nsociety and fulfil their caring role.  \n \nThe aim for the grant opportunity is to ensure carers? wellbeing is maintained or improved \nwith the supports and services through this program funding. \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \nEfficiency \n \nEffectiveness \n \nEquity \n \nQuality \n \nDepartmental outcome areas \nThe purpose of the department is to support the Government to lead and shape Australia?s \nhealth, disability and aged care systems and outcomes through evidence-based policy, well \ntargeted programs, and best practice regulation. \nThe department?s Annual Report 2024-25 outlines for carers:   \nOutcome 4: Disability and Carers  \nSupporting the independence of people with disability and carers by providing targeted \nsupports. \nProgram 4.1: Disability and Carers \nProgram Objective: To support people with disabilities and carers to actively participate in \ncommunity and economic life. \n\n \nCarer Gateway Outcomes Framework \n3 \nKey Activity: 4.1A Disability and Carer Support  \nPerformance Measure: Extent to which carers who are registered with Carer Gateway local \nservice providers? wellbeing is assessed as improved. \nThe current performance measure for Integrated Carer Support Service (ICSS) is assessed \nusing 2 targets:  \nTarget 4.1A(a) \n? \nPercentage (at least 30%) of carers registered with Carer Gateway local service \nproviders assessed as having improved carer wellbeing in the current reporting \nperiod. \nTarget 4.1A(b) \n? \nPercentage (at least 35%) of carers registered with Carer Gateway local service \nproviders assessed as having improved level of carer wellbeing since the program \ncommenced. \nPolicy Outcome Areas  \nThe National Carer Strategy 2024-2034 sets out a national agenda to support and improve \nthe lives of Australia?s unpaid carers and includes 6 priority outcome areas to achieve the \nStrategy?s vision and objectives. Priorities 2-5 directly align to the vision and objectives for \nCarer Gateway:  \n2. Carers can access supports, services and programs at the right time, right place and \nin the right way.  \n3. Ensure carers are able to develop knowledge and skills when needed to fulfill their \ncaring role.  \n4. Carers can access and participate in employment and education or training, \nincluding to improve their financial well-being.   \n5. Carers have access to supports that safeguard their psychological, physical and \nsocial wellbeing.  \n \n \n\n \nCarer Gateway Outcomes Framework \n4 \nThe National Carer Strategy Action Plan 2024-2027 makes 6 commitments. Initial efforts will \nfocus on Action Plan Commitment 1 which aims to support the wellbeing of carers by \nreviewing and improving:  \na. specific services offered through Carer Gateway  \nb. the accessibility and inclusivity of Carer Gateway.  \nCommitment 1 will be delivered via 10 actions, which will:  \n? \nupskill and build capacity in the unpaid carer sector service delivery to ensure that \ncarers? unique needs are addressed and improve national service outcomes  \n? \nsupport carers? physical and mental health, safety, wellbeing and financial security  \n? \naddress barriers to accessing services including respite and mental health support \nfaced by all carer cohorts, with consideration to the unique needs of First Nations, \nCALD, LGBTQIA+, older, young, and defence family and veteran?s carers, and carers \nwith disability.  \nProgram Goal  \nThe goal for the Carer Gateway program is to:  \nImprove unpaid carer wellbeing, increase their capacity and support their                             \nparticipation, socially and economically, through prioritised investment in effective \npreventative support services.  \nOutcomes \nThe Program Logic and Outcomes Framework sets out how the following three long-term \noutcomes will be achieved and how progress towards achieving them will be measured:  \nOutcome 1: Carer Gateway supports and services are inclusive, accessible and appropriate \nto unpaid carers needs and delivered in a timely manner. \nOutcome 2: Carer Gateway supports and services have positive effects on wellbeing, \nincluding social and economic participation.  \nOutcome 3: Carer Gateway supports and services prioritise preventative, carer centred \nsupport services which are shown through evidence to be efficient and effective.  \nThese are the outcomes the grant opportunity expects service providers to achieve by the \nend of the grant round by 2031.  \n\n \nCarer Gateway Outcomes Framework \n5 \nMonitoring Outcomes \nProgram monitoring, reporting and evaluation activities will be undertaken by the \ndepartment. A specialist organisation may be engaged to administer or contribute to \nreporting against the outcomes framework, measuring and reporting on the performance \nof organisations engaged under the ICSS.  \nSpecified measures may be represented in a variety of publicly available and internal \nreports. This includes but is not limited to; Bi-annual Performance Monitoring Reports, \nEvaluation Reports and Carer Wellbeing Survey Reports.\n\n \nCarer Gateway Outcomes Framework \n6 \nProgram Logic Model \nProgram goal: Improve unpaid carer wellbeing, increase their capacity and support their participation, socially and economically, through prioritised investment in effective preventative support services.  \nInputs  \nFunding and other resources \n \nActivities \nWhat we will do \nOutputs \nDeliverable products \n \nShort-term Outcomes \n(Immediate) \nDirect program attribution and responsibility \nMedium-term Outcomes \n(Intermediate) \nBehavioural change \nLong-term Outcomes \n(Program Objectives ? Efficiency, \nEffectiveness, Equity & Quality) \nSystem change \nPolicy direction  \n? \nNational Carer Strategy   \n? \nSupporting Action Plans   \n \nImpact Evaluation of the Integrated \nCarer Support Service (Carer Gateway) \nReport   \n  \nInquiry into the Recognition of \nUnpaid\u202fCarers Report\u202f  \n   \nService Design   \n? \nGrant Opportunity Guidelines \n(GOGs)   \n  \nPerformance monitoring  \n? \nKey Performance Indicator \nFramework   \n   \nEvidence-based tools and practice \napproaches  \n  \nFunding  \n   \nNational Infrastructure  \n  \nState/ territory infrastructure   \n   \nLocal Infrastructure   \n  \nPartnerships/ Networks  \n   \nNational communications activities   \n   \nProvider Organisations  \n  \nStaff   \n   \nTraining   \n \n  \nCGSPs promote local Carer Gateway \nservices.  \n  \nHigh-quality providers with a diversity \nof expertise deliver Carer Gateway \nsupports and services to eligible unpaid \ncarers.   \n  \nSupports and services delivered \nthrough Carer Gateway are:   \n? \nhigh-quality   \n? \nappropriate   \n? \nculturally safe   \n? \ncarer-centred   \n? \nequitable  \n? \ntailored    \n? \ninclusive   \n? \naccessible   \n? \nlocal   \n? \nintegrated  \n? \ntimely   \n? \nresponsive.    \n  \nCGSPs deliver on the Government?s \nexpectations to support the well-being \nof carers through piloting \nrecommendations to review and \nimprove:   \n? \nspecific services offered through \nCarer Gateway  \n? \nthe accessibility and inclusivity of \nCarer Gateway.   \n \nThe department undertakes program \nmanagement and assurance processes \nto manage and improve provider \nperformance and compliance.   \n  \nInformation/  \nAdvice/ Referral  \n   \nIntake and assessment  \n  \nCarer support planning  \n  \nTailored Support Packages \nincluding:  \n? \none-off practical support  \n? \ncarer directed packages, \nincluding access to \nplanned respite and carer \ntraining   \n  \nAssistance with accessing \nemergency respite care  \n  \nCounselling  \n  \nCoaching  \n \nPre-employment support \n   \nPeer support   \n  \nReporting   \n  \nService and performance data \n  \n  \n \n  \nIncreased awareness of carers (and family/ friends/ \ncommunity) role and the support and services available \nto carers through Carer Gateway. \nIncreased uptake of Carer Gateway services \nand supports.  \nCarer Gateway supports and \nservices are inclusive, accessible \nand appropriate to carer needs \nand delivered in a timely manner. \nCarers have appropriate access to supports and services \nbased on their level of need, where they live and who \nthey are.  \nCarers can access supports and services that are \ninclusive, flexible, timely, and tailored to their unique \nneeds.  \nProviders and their networks deliver tailored supports \nand services that meet the unique needs of carers in \neach service region for national coverage.   \nCarers are supported with improved \nlinkages and referral pathways to other \nmainstream and complementary services.  \nCarers have access to relevant information to make \ninformed decisions about supports and services \navailable to them.  \nCarers improve their psychological, physical \nand social wellbeing as a result of accessing \nCarer Gateway services.  \nCarer Gateway supports and \nservices have positive effects on \nwellbeing, including social and \neconomic participation. \nEnhanced Carer Gateway supports for carers to access \nemployment and education or training, including to \nimprove their financial wellbeing.  \nCarers are able to continue caring post crisis.  \nIncrease in carers? self-confidence reported \nby carers. \nCarers choose their goals and preferences using \nevidence-based tools to guide need.  \nCarer Gateway supports carers to maintain \nresilience and continue in their caring role.  \nCarers are supported in a crisis and referred to \nappropriate supports.  \nIncrease uptake of existing carer training and coaching \nofferings.  \nCarers proactively plan for and engage with \nsupports and services (before crisis \noccurs).  \nCarer Gateway supports and \nservices prioritise preventative, \ncarer centred support services \nwhich are shown through \nevidence to be efficient and \neffective. \n \nSupports and services are prioritised towards carers \nmost in need.   \nInvestment is prioritised in low-cost, preventative \nservices.   \nImproved Carer Gateway supports for carers to develop \nknowledge and skills when needed to fulfil their caring \nrole through professional and peer support.  \nIncrease of inclusive, flexible, and tailored respite models \nincluding emergency crisis support.  \nReduce reliance on emergency respite \ncare.\u202f  \n \nThe department has access to client and service data to \nevaluate effectiveness of services.  \n \nProviders demonstrate continuous quality \nimprovement to meet the needs of unpaid \ncarers.   \n\n \nCarer Gateway Outcomes Framework \n7 \nCarer Gateway Outcomes Framework \nOutcome 1: Carer Gateway supports and services are inclusive, accessible and appropriate to unpaid carers needs and delivered in a timely manner. \nShort to medium-term outcome \nIndicators \nMeasure \nData source \nMedium-term: Increased uptake of \nCarer Gateway services and \nsupports. \nIncrease in traffic and interaction with supports and \nservices through the Carer Gateway \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \nIncrease uptake of Carer Gateway services and supports \nby under-represented cohorts ? improved reach \n# of carers registered with Carer Gateway service providers  \n \n# of carers accessing Carer Gateway services  \n \n \n% increase in carers accessing Carer Gateway services \n \n# of carers with an initial carers assessment  \n \nemerging carer groups within service region  \n \n# of out of scope requests / % approved \nData Exchange (DEX) \n \nDEX  \nCarer Wellbeing Survey (CWS)  \n \nDEX \n \nDEX \n \nActivity Work Plan Reports (AWP) \n \nOther (OTH)  \nShort-term: Increased awareness of \ncarers (and family/ friends/ \ncommunity) role and the support \nand services available to carers \nthrough Carer Gateway. \n \nIncrease in awareness of the supports and services \navailable for unpaid carers through Carer Gateway   \n \nPromotional activities and referrals are increasing reach to \nunpaid carers \n \n \n \nUptake and Reach ? initial engagements \n \n \n \n \nUptake and Reach ? reconnect with carers \n \n \n# (quantity) of promotion and networking activities undertaken  \n \n% individuals identifying as carers \n \n# of carer clients who receive planned services  \n \nnew carer uptake rate (never received service before) \n \ninstances of support delivered (focus on information/advice/referral and \nintake & assessment)  \n \nclients receiving service 12 months from last service \n \n \nAWP \n \nCWS  \n \nDEX \n \nDEX \n \nDEX \n \nDEX \n\n \nCarer Gateway Outcomes Framework \n8 \nShort to medium-term outcome \nIndicators \nMeasure \nData source \nShort-term: Carers have appropriate \naccess to supports and services \nbased on their level of need, where \nthey live and who they are. \nEquity ? national consistency  \n \n \n \n \nEquity ? inclusive supports  \n \n \nService Quality and Effectiveness ? timely and responsive \nService Quality and Effectiveness ? provider capability \nIncreased expertise and partnering for service delivery \nofferings for specific cohorts \n \n \n \nIncrease in the diversity of carers accessing services due to \nincreased cultural appropriateness \n \n# of carers identifying as a carer [should be 100% for Carer Gateway clients \n/ vs all department programs]  \n \n% increase in the number of carers identifying as a carer \n \n% of carers serviced from relevant carer population estimates \n \nKPI 8 ? % proportion of carers accessing Carer Gateway services from \nvulnerable and disadvantaged cohorts compared to relevant population \nestimates for the respective cohorts \n \n# of support service instances delivered for each service type  \n \nKPI 3 - % of carers with assessed satisfaction \n \nKPI 4 ? % proportion of carer clients achieving high satisfaction  \n \nKPI 1 ? call wait times for the national 1800 number \n \ntraining and accreditations for staff \n \ncompliance with minimum qualifications for staff  \n \nPM ? # of Carer Gateway clients accessing supports and services by \ndemographic including gender, First Nations status, culturally and \nlinguistically diverse (CALD), remoteness.  \n \nKPI 8 ? % proportion of Carer Gateway clients accessing supports and \nservices by demographic including gender, First Nations status, CALD, \nremoteness.  \nDEX \n \n \nDEX \n \nDEX \n \nDEX \n \n \nDEX \n \nDEX \n \nDEX \n \nAWP \n \nAWP \n \nAWP  \n \nDEX \n \n \n \nDEX \n\n \nCarer Gateway Outcomes Framework \n9 \nShort to medium-term outcome \nIndicators \nMeasure \nData source \nShort-term: Carers can access \nsupports and services that are \ninclusive, flexible, timely, and tailored \nto their unique needs. \nCarers are given opportunity to engage in a needs \nassessment and support planning process  \n \n \n \nService offerings are carer centred informed b
