2026-09-23T21:06:34.199546Z [] {'source_reference_id': 'HSNSW_6108', 'source_id': 'buynsw', 'title': 'Cleaning Automation Initiative EOI (Phase 1)', 'source_url': None, 'issuing_agency': 'HealthShare NSW', 'category': 'EOI (Open)', 'status': 'open', 'publish_date': '2026-09-09', 'closing_date': '2026-10-01', 'value_amount': None, 'value_currency': None, 'value_notes': 'Suppliers should provide indicative commercial information, including: Capital purchase cost for equipment. Leasing or as-a-service options, including commercial structure. Battery replacement costs and lifecycle. Consumable costs, including brushes, filters, and cleaning agents. Expected asset life and depreciation considerations. End-of-life disposal or recycling arrangements. Warranty terms and conditions. Any implementation or onboarding costs. HSNSW is seeking flexibility in commercial structures to support both initial evaluation and longer-term adoption, subject to performance, value for money, and organisational requirements.', 'location': 'Westmead Hospital, Royal North Shore Hospital, Tamworth Hospital, Wagga Wagga Base Hospital, Southeast Regional Hospital (Bega), Nepean Hospital', 'description': "HealthShare NSW, on behalf of NSW Health, is conducting an Expression of Interest (EOI) for autonomous cleaning technologies, specifically autonomous floor scrubbers and vacuum cleaners. This EOI is the first phase of a three-stage procurement process aimed at understanding market capability, assessing technology maturity, evaluating suitability for healthcare environments, and identifying suppliers for a structured trial program. The overall project objectives include evaluating cleaning automation, supporting workforce sustainability by reducing repetitive tasks in non-clinical areas, improving operational efficiency, supporting innovation, and informing future procurement decisions.\n\nSuppliers are invited to propose solutions for autonomous floor scrubbers and/or autonomous vacuum cleaners. The EOI will lead to a shortlist of suppliers for Phase 2, which involves a 4-month placement trial program at multiple NSW Health facilities. Proposed trial sites include Westmead Hospital, Royal North Shore Hospital, Tamworth Hospital, Wagga Wagga Base Hospital, Southeast Regional Hospital (Bega), and Nepean Hospital, with equipment trialed for 2 months at each of two selected locations. HealthShare NSW's preference is for trial equipment to be provided at no cost, though purchase may be considered if a no-cost trial is unavailable. Following successful trials, Phase 3 may involve a limited Request for Tender (RFT) process to establish a supplier panel for 3 years, with two potential 12-month extensions.\n\nThe EOI was published on 9 September 2026, with an amended closing date of 1 October 2026 at 3:00 PM. Evaluation of submissions is scheduled from 28 September to 16 October 2026, with ICT and Digital Security assessments in October-November 2026. The target start date for trials is February 2027, and the target date for the Phase 3 RFT is 2 July 2027. Suppliers must provide a separate and complete response for each proposed machine or model. The procurement category has a heightened risk of modern slavery, and suppliers will be asked to provide relevant information. The period for questions and clarifications has closed, and respondents are strictly prohibited from contacting agency representatives outside the specified process.", 'summary_headline': 'HealthShare NSW seeks Expressions of Interest for autonomous cleaning technologies for NSW Health facilities.', 'contact_name': 'Murray Affleck', 'contact_email': 'Murray.Affleck@health.nsw.gov.au', 'contact_phone': '02 8644 2528', 'lodgment_address': 'https://buy.nsw.gov.au/opportunity', 'documents': [{'file_name': 'HSNSW_6108---Addendum-1.pdf.txt', 'extracted_text': "HealthShare NSW \n \n \n \n \n1 Reserve Road, St Leonards NSW 2065 \n(02) 8644 2000 \nLocked Bag 2030, St Leonards NSW 1590 \nhealthshare.nsw.gov.au \n \n \n \n \n \n24 September 2026 \nAddendum #1  \nHSNSW_6108 - Cleaning Automation Initiative EOI (Phase 1) \nHealthShare NSW, on behalf Agency, publishes this Amendment and Clarification to the above Request for \nExpression of Intrest. This addendum consists of 3 page(s) and now forms part of the RFT documents.  \nRespondents are responsible for ensuring they have:  \ndownloaded all addenda;  \n? \nconsidered the contents and included those factors in developing an offer;  \n? \nconfirm all addenda have been downloaded in their submitted response.  \n? \nSubmitted responses that do not include this information may be deemed non-complying and \npotentially excluded from consideration (at the sole discretion of the Agency).  \nThe period for questions and/or clarifications has now closed.  \nLate tenders may not be accepted Sincerely, \n \nAmendment # 1  \n  \nRefer to file HSNSW_6108 - EOI Part A ? Terms, Conditions and Requirements \n  \n8.2 Closing Date and Time \n  \nClause 8.2 currently reads:  Closing Date 25 September 2026 @ 3pm \n  \nClause 8.2 shall now read:  Closing Date 1 October 2026 @ 3pm \n \n \n \n \n\nClarification 1  \n1. \nTrial commercial arrangements \nQ: Part A indicates that equipment selected for the trial may be purchased, while Part B asks suppliers to \nconfirm whether equipment can be provided at no cost. Could you please clarify the intended commercial \narrangement and whether a fully supplier-funded trial is a condition of shortlisting? \nA: HealthShare NSW's preference is for trial equipment to be provided at no cost. This is not a condition \nof shortlisting. Purchase may only be considered where a no-cost trial is unavailable, and a trial is \nrequired to validate a solution assessed as suitable through the EOI.  \n2. \nEquipment-specific response requirements  \nQ: The EOI requirements appear to require suppliers to provide information for each individual machine or \nequipment model proposed. Could you please clarify which questions, sections or schedules must be \nanswered separately for each machine? \nIn particular, should each proposed machine provide separate responses covering: \nTechnical specifications and operating capabilities; \nCleaning performance and suitability for the proposed trial areas; \nFloor-surface compatibility; \nDimensions, operating width, turning radius and access requirements; \nBattery capacity, charging requirements and operating time; \nWater, chemical and consumable requirements; \nInfection prevention and chemical compatibility; \nSafety features and operational controls; \nCybersecurity, data, connectivity and system-integration capabilities; \nTraining, implementation and support requirements; \nMaintenance, repairs, warranty and spare-parts arrangements; \nTrial KPIs, performance measures and reporting capabilities; \nPricing or commercial arrangements; and \nAny other requirements identified in Part B or the relevant response schedules? \nAlternatively, may suppliers provide one consolidated response for common information and identify only \nthe differences between each proposed machine? Please also confirm whether separate pricing, \ncompliance statements, supporting evidence and trial plans are required for every machine.  \n \nA: Where multiple pieces of equipment or models are proposed, a separate and complete response is \nrequired for each machine or model, including all applicable EOI requirements and response schedules.  \n  \n3. \nTrial locations, area sizes and floor surfaces \nQ: For each of the six proposed trial locations?Westmead Hospital, Royal North Shore Hospital, \nTamworth Hospital, Wagga Wagga Base Hospital, Southeast Regional Hospital in Bega, and Nepean \nHospital?could you please provide the following information: \nThe approximate size or size range of the areas to be cleaned; \nThe types and approximate proportions of floor surfaces, such as vinyl, tiles, polished concrete, carpet or \nother materials; \nThe width of the narrowest corridors, doorways or access points; \nWhether the trial areas include ramps, lifts, glass walls, high-traffic areas or other operating constraints; \nThe proposed cleaning hours and any restrictions on robot operation; and \nThe availability of charging points, water supply, drainage and secure equipment-storage facilities. \nThis information will help us recommend the most appropriate machine models and configurations for \neach trial location. \nA: The requested site-specific information is not currently available at this level of detail. Suppliers \nshould base their response on the information provided in the EOI and propose equipment suitable for a \nrange of hospital environments and floor surfaces. Specific site conditions and requirements will be \nconfirmed during trial planning and site assessment. \nThe trial locations have been identified to provide a range of hospital environments and operating \nconditions. A wide variety of floor surfaces and environments are present at each site and across NSW \nHealth facilities more broadly, including different types of vinyl, tiles, polished concrete, carpet and other \nfloor finishes. The specific floor types, areas and operating conditions will vary between locations. \n\n4. \nInfection prevention and chemical compatibility \nQ: Could you please identify the specific eWater products and any other cleaning or disinfection \nchemicals that the equipment must accommodate, including the relevant concentrations, pH levels and \ntechnical specifications? \n \nA: The reference to eWater was included in error and should be disregarded. Suppliers should identify the \ncleaning and disinfection chemicals or consumables recommended or required for their proposed \nsolution. Proposed products must be suitable for use in a hospital environment and meet hospital-grade \nproducts. As identified by the Therapeutic Goods Administration (TGA). \n \n \nQ: Could you also confirm what evidence of chemical compatibility and infection prevention, and control \nvalidation is expected at the EOI stage? \n \nA: No specific evidence or formal validation documentation is required at the EOI stage. Suppliers should \nprovide details of any infection prevention and control testing undertaken for the proposed solution, \nincluding how the equipment has been tested and the basis for any claims regarding its cleaning \nperformance and effectiveness. Suppliers should also identify any applicable standards or testing \nprotocols used \n \n5. \nTrial evaluation and performance measures \nQ: Are proposed trial KPIs, performance measures or assessment protocols available to assist suppliers in \naligning their deployment and reporting plans with HealthShare NSW?s objectives? \nIn particular, will the assessment consider cleaning quality, effective area coverage, task completion, \nmanual intervention, equipment downtime, water and consumable usage, safety incidents and staff \nfeedback? \n \nA: HealthShare NSW intends to assess the trial against agreed performance measures covering cleaning \nquality, cleaning coverage and task completion, manual intervention, autonomous utilisation, equipment \nreliability and downtime, water and consumable usage, safety, staff time savings and staff feedback. \nThe trial is expected to include objective data collected through equipment telemetry, together with site \nobservations and cleaning assessments. \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n\n"}, {'file_name': 'HSNSW_6108---Addendum-1.txt', 'extracted_text': "HealthShare NSW \n \n \n \n \n1 Reserve Road, St Leonards NSW 2065 \n(02) 8644 2000 \nLocked Bag 2030, St Leonards NSW 1590 \nhealthshare.nsw.gov.au \n \n \n \n \n \n24 September 2026 \nAddendum #1  \nHSNSW_6108 - Cleaning Automation Initiative EOI (Phase 1) \nHealthShare NSW, on behalf Agency, publishes this Amendment and Clarification to the above Request for \nExpression of Intrest. This addendum consists of 3 page(s) and now forms part of the RFT documents.  \nRespondents are responsible for ensuring they have:  \ndownloaded all addenda;  \n? \nconsidered the contents and included those factors in developing an offer;  \n? \nconfirm all addenda have been downloaded in their submitted response.  \n? \nSubmitted responses that do not include this information may be deemed non-complying and \npotentially excluded from consideration (at the sole discretion of the Agency).  \nThe period for questions and/or clarifications has now closed.  \nLate tenders may not be accepted Sincerely, \n \nAmendment # 1  \n  \nRefer to file HSNSW_6108 - EOI Part A ? Terms, Conditions and Requirements \n  \n8.2 Closing Date and Time \n  \nClause 8.2 currently reads:  Closing Date 25 September 2026 @ 3pm \n  \nClause 8.2 shall now read:  Closing Date 1 October 2026 @ 3pm \n \n \n \n \n\nClarification 1  \n1. \nTrial commercial arrangements \nQ: Part A indicates that equipment selected for the trial may be purchased, while Part B asks suppliers to \nconfirm whether equipment can be provided at no cost. Could you please clarify the intended commercial \narrangement and whether a fully supplier-funded trial is a condition of shortlisting? \nA: HealthShare NSW's preference is for trial equipment to be provided at no cost. This is not a condition \nof shortlisting. Purchase may only be considered where a no-cost trial is unavailable, and a trial is \nrequired to validate a solution assessed as suitable through the EOI.  \n2. \nEquipment-specific response requirements  \nQ: The EOI requirements appear to require suppliers to provide information for each individual machine or \nequipment model proposed. Could you please clarify which questions, sections or schedules must be \nanswered separately for each machine? \nIn particular, should each proposed machine provide separate responses covering: \nTechnical specifications and operating capabilities; \nCleaning performance and suitability for the proposed trial areas; \nFloor-surface compatibility; \nDimensions, operating width, turning radius and access requirements; \nBattery capacity, charging requirements and operating time; \nWater, chemical and consumable requirements; \nInfection prevention and chemical compatibility; \nSafety features and operational controls; \nCybersecurity, data, connectivity and system-integration capabilities; \nTraining, implementation and support requirements; \nMaintenance, repairs, warranty and spare-parts arrangements; \nTrial KPIs, performance measures and reporting capabilities; \nPricing or commercial arrangements; and \nAny other requirements identified in Part B or the relevant response schedules? \nAlternatively, may suppliers provide one consolidated response for common information and identify only \nthe differences between each proposed machine? Please also confirm whether separate pricing, \ncompliance statements, supporting evidence and trial plans are required for every machine.  \n \nA: Where multiple pieces of equipment or models are proposed, a separate and complete response is \nrequired for each machine or model, including all applicable EOI requirements and response schedules.  \n  \n3. \nTrial locations, area sizes and floor surfaces \nQ: For each of the six proposed trial locations?Westmead Hospital, Royal North Shore Hospital, \nTamworth Hospital, Wagga Wagga Base Hospital, Southeast Regional Hospital in Bega, and Nepean \nHospital?could you please provide the following information: \nThe approximate size or size range of the areas to be cleaned; \nThe types and approximate proportions of floor surfaces, such as vinyl, tiles, polished concrete, carpet or \nother materials; \nThe width of the narrowest corridors, doorways or access points; \nWhether the trial areas include ramps, lifts, glass walls, high-traffic areas or other operating constraints; \nThe proposed cleaning hours and any restrictions on robot operation; and \nThe availability of charging points, water supply, drainage and secure equipment-storage facilities. \nThis information will help us recommend the most appropriate machine models and configurations for \neach trial location. \nA: The requested site-specific information is not currently available at this level of detail. Suppliers \nshould base their response on the information provided in the EOI and propose equipment suitable for a \nrange of hospital environments and floor surfaces. Specific site conditions and requirements will be \nconfirmed during trial planning and site assessment. \nThe trial locations have been identified to provide a range of hospital environments and operating \nconditions. A wide variety of floor surfaces and environments are present at each site and across NSW \nHealth facilities more broadly, including different types of vinyl, tiles, polished concrete, carpet and other \nfloor finishes. The specific floor types, areas and operating conditions will vary between locations. \n\n4. \nInfection prevention and chemical compatibility \nQ: Could you please identify the specific eWater products and any other cleaning or disinfection \nchemicals that the equipment must accommodate, including the relevant concentrations, pH levels and \ntechnical specifications? \n \nA: The reference to eWater was included in error and should be disregarded. Suppliers should identify the \ncleaning and disinfection chemicals or consumables recommended or required for their proposed \nsolution. Proposed products must be suitable for use in a hospital environment and meet hospital-grade \nproducts. As identified by the Therapeutic Goods Administration (TGA). \n \n \nQ: Could you also confirm what evidence of chemical compatibility and infection prevention, and control \nvalidation is expected at the EOI stage? \n \nA: No specific evidence or formal validation documentation is required at the EOI stage. Suppliers should \nprovide details of any infection prevention and control testing undertaken for the proposed solution, \nincluding how the equipment has been tested and the basis for any claims regarding its cleaning \nperformance and effectiveness. Suppliers should also identify any applicable standards or testing \nprotocols used \n \n5. \nTrial evaluation and performance measures \nQ: Are proposed trial KPIs, performance measures or assessment protocols available to assist suppliers in \naligning their deployment and reporting plans with HealthShare NSW?s objectives? \nIn particular, will the assessment consider cleaning quality, effective area coverage, task completion, \nmanual intervention, equipment downtime, water and consumable usage, safety incidents and staff \nfeedback? \n \nA: HealthShare NSW intends to assess the trial against agreed performance measures covering cleaning \nquality, cleaning coverage and task completion, manual intervention, autonomous utilisation, equipment \nreliability and downtime, water and consumable usage, safety, staff time savings and staff feedback. \nThe trial is expected to include objective data collected through equipment telemetry, together with site \nobservations and cleaning assessments. \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n\n"}, {'file_name': 'HSNSW_6108---EOI-Part-A---Terms-Conditions-and-Requirements.pdf.txt', 'extracted_text': " \n \nOFFICIAL: Sensitive ? NSW Government \nOFFICIAL: Sensitive ? NSW Government \n \n \nHealthShare NSW \nHealthShare NSW invites this Expression of Interest for and on behalf of \nNSW Health Administration Corporation (HAC) \n \n \n \n \n \n \n  \nExpression of Interest (EOI) \nPart A - Terms & Conditions & Requirements \n  \n \nNo. \nHSNSW_6108 \nName \nCleaning Automation Initiative \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \nwww.health.nsw.gov.au \n\n \nPage | 2  \nOFFICIAL: Sensitive ? NSW Government \nOFFICIAL: Sensitive ? NSW Government \nCONTENTS \nSection A \nEOI Terms and Conditions \n3 \n1. \nIntroduction \n3 \n2. \nContext \n3 \n3. \nProcurement Process \n4 \n4. \nEOI Structure \n5 \n5. \nRequirements Overview \n6 \n6. \nGoods and Services Sought \n6 \n7. \nStatement of Requirements \n6 \n8. \nEOI Instructions and Key Information \n12 \n9. \nEvaluation Criteria \n13 \n10. \nTerms and Conditions of Participation \n15 \n11. \nLegal entity \n16 \n12. \nDiscontinuance of EOI Process \n16 \n13. \nEthics \n16 \n14. \nLodgement of Responses \n17 \n15. \nEvaluation \n17 \n\n \nPage | 3  \nOFFICIAL: Sensitive ? NSW Government \nOFFICIAL: Sensitive ? NSW Government \nSection A \nEOI Terms and Conditions \n1. \nIntroduction \n1.1 \nAgency Details \nThis Expression of Interest (EOI) is issued by HealthShare NSW on behalf of the Agency \nAgency details \n \nAgency name \nHealthShare NSW \nAgency address \n1 Reserve Road, St Leonards NSW 2065 \nHealthShare NSW (HSNSW) is a unit within the Health Administration Corporation and provides shared \nservices, including shared procurement services, to NSW Health agencies. HSNSW is responsible for the \nconduct of this EOI, assisted by the Agency. References to the ?Agency? will encompass references to the \nHealth Administration Corporation, HSNSW and NSW Health as appropriate. \n2. \nContext \n2.1 \nBackground \nHSNSW provides environmental cleaning services across a large and diverse portfolio of public health \nfacilities throughout New South Wales. \nIn 2023, HSNSW undertook a trial of autonomous floor scrubbing technology within a hospital \nenvironment to evaluate the potential benefits of cleaning automation. Outcomes from this trial identified \nopportunities to improve cleaning consistency, enhance workforce utilisation, reduce repetitive manual \nhandling activities, and improve visibility of cleaning activities through digital reporting and telemetry. \nSince completion of the trial, autonomous cleaning technologies have continued to mature considerably. \nHSNSW is now seeking to assess a broader range of cleaning automation solutions, including autonomous \nfloor scrubbers, autonomous vacuum cleaners. \nThis Expression of Interest forms the first stage of a three-stage procurement process intended to: \n? \nUnderstand current market capability. \n? \nAssess the maturity of available technologies. \n? \nEvaluate suitability for healthcare environments. \n? \nIdentify suppliers for participation in a structured trial program. \n? \nInform future procurement and implementation decisions. \n2.2 \nOverall Project Objectives \nEvaluate Cleaning Automation Technologies \nAssess the effectiveness, usability, safety, reliability, and operational suitability of autonomous cleaning \ntechnologies in healthcare environments. \nSupport Workforce Sustainability \nInvestigate how automation may support existing cleaning services in non-clinical areas by reducing \nrepetitive manual tasks. It also supports staff to perform environmental cleaning in higher-risk clinical \nareas, ensuring compliance with the minimum cleaning standards outlined in the Policy Directive \nPD2023_018. \n \nImprove Operational Efficiency \n\n \n \nPage | 4  \nOFFICIAL: Sensitive ? NSW Government \nOFFICIAL: Sensitive ? NSW Government \nAssess opportunities to improve cleaning consistency, auditability, productivity, and service \nresponsiveness. \nSupport Innovation and Future Readiness \nIdentify technologies that may support future NSW Health service delivery models and hospital \ninfrastructure planning. \nInform Future Procurement \nGenerate evidence-based findings to support future procurement decisions relating to cleaning \nautomation technologies. \n \n3. \nProcurement Process \n3.1 \nOverview \nThe procurement process will follow a multi-stage approach: \n? \nPhase 1: Expression of Interest (EOI) \n? \nPhase 2: Placement Trials \n? \nPhase 3: Request for Tender (RFT) \n \n3.2 \nPhase 1 ? The EOI \nThe purpose of the EOI is to identify suppliers with suitable autonomous cleaning technologies and \ncapability to participate in a trial program. \nSuppliers may respond to one or more of the following technology categories: \n? \nAutonomous floor scrubbers \n? \nAutonomous vacuum cleaners \nResponses will be assessed against the evaluation criteria detailed in this EOI to establish a shortlist of \nsuppliers for Phase 2.  \n3.3 \nPhase 2 ? Placement Trials \nHSNSW intends to undertake a structured 4-month trial program involving selected suppliers and \ntechnologies at multiple NSW Health facilities as identified below in section 4.1. \nIt is anticipated if selected equipment will undertake trials for 2 months at each location across two \nselected locations. \nThe purpose of the trial program is to evaluate: \n? \nOperational suitability \n? \nCleaning performance \n? \nSafety \n? \nReliability \n? \nStaff usability \n? \nMaintenance requirements \n? \nReporting capability \n? \nScalability across healthcare environments \nFindings from the trial program will inform future procurement and supplier panel decisions. \n \n \n\n \n \nPage | 5  \nOFFICIAL: Sensitive ? NSW Government \nOFFICIAL: Sensitive ? NSW Government \n3.4 \nPhase 3 -Request for Tender (RFT) \nSubject to successful completion of the trial phase and approval to proceed, HSNSW may conduct a \nlimited tender process to establish a supplier panel for cleaning automation technologies. \nThe RFT will include detailed specifications, pricing schedules, and contractual terms and conditions. \nOnly suppliers shortlisted from the EOI phase will be eligible to participate in this subsequent stage. \n4. \nEOI Structure \nThis EOI comprises the following documents: \n? \nPart A Terms & Conditions and Requirements \n? \nPart B Response Schedules \nEOI responses must contain the completed Schedules which are included in the document ?Part B \nResponse Schedules?.  \nResponses should focus on demonstrating organisational capability, relevant experience, technical \nmaturity, implementation approach, and suitability of proposed solutions for healthcare environments. \nNote that any variation from the prescribed format in the respondent?s compulsory compliant response \nmay result in the Response being excluded from evaluation. \n4.1 \nScope of the EOI \nThe purpose of this EOI is to identify and assess suitably qualified suppliers capable of providing \nautonomous cleaning technologies suitable for deployment within NSW Health facilities. \nHSNSW intends to evaluate a range of autonomous cleaning solutions to support future decision-making \nregarding potential implementation across healthcare environments. \nSuppliers may respond for one or more of the following technology categories: \n? \nAutonomous Floor Scrubbers: Autonomous or semi-autonomous floor cleaning systems capable of \nperforming routine floor scrubbing activities within healthcare facilities. \n? \nAutonomous Vacuum Cleaners: Autonomous or semi-autonomous vacuum-cleaning systems suitable \nfor commercial and healthcare environments. \nThrough this EOI, HSNSW seeks to: \n? \nUnderstand the current market capability and available technologies. \n? \nIdentify suppliers with demonstrated experience delivering autonomous cleaning solutions. \n? \nAssess the maturity and reliability of available systems. \n? \nUnderstand deployment, support and maintenance requirements. \n? \nAssess suitability for healthcare environments. \n? \nExplore commercial delivery models, including purchase & leasing arrangements. \n? \nIdentify suppliers suitable for participation in a multi-site trial program.  \n \nProposed sites are; \no Westmead Hospital,  \no Royal North Shore Hospital, \no Tamworth Hospital,  \no Wagga Wagga Base Hospital, \no Southeast Regional Hospital (Bega),  \no Nepean Hospital. \n \n\n \n \nPage | 6  \nOFFICIAL: Sensitive ? NSW Government \nOFFICIAL: Sensitive ? NSW Government \nResponses should focus on demonstrating capability and suitability rather than providing detailed pricing \nsubmissions. \n \n4.2 \nExpected Contract & Duration of RFT \nStanding Offer Agreement \nThe Agency proposes to enter into the proposed Agreement with the successful tenderers to establish a \npanel of suppliers for the Goods and Services.  \nIt is envisaged that the panel will be in place for a term of three (3) years, which may be extended for two \n(2) x twelve (12) month options.  \n \n5. \nRequirements Overview \n5.1 \nHSNSW is seeking information from suppliers capable of providing autonomous and operator-assisted \ncleaning technologies suitable for healthcare environments. The purpose of this request is to assess \nmarket capability, operational suitability, and alignment with NSW Health requirements, including safety, \ninfection control, workforce integration, and digital security. \n5.2 \nSuppliers should describe available operating modes, including autonomous, supervised, assisted, manual, \nremote-control, or other intervention capabilities where available. \n6. \nGoods and Services Sought \n6.1 \nSuppliers may respond for one or more technology categories, including: \n6.1.1 \nAutonomous Floor Scrubbers \n(a) Solutions capable of performing autonomous and operator-assisted floor scrubbing and wet cleaning \nactivities within hospital and healthcare environments. \n6.1.2 \nAutonomous Vacuum Cleaners \n(a) Solutions capable of performing autonomous and operator-assisted dry vacuuming and floor \nmaintenance activities within hospital and healthcare environments. \n7. \nStatement of Requirements \nHSNSW seeks cleaning technologies capable of operating safely, effectively, and reliably within complex \nhealthcare environments, including public and non-clinical areas. Solutions must support both routine \ncleaning and responsive cleaning activities when required. \nSuppliers must describe how their solution meets the following capability areas. \n7.1 \nFunctional Capability and Operational Control \n(a) Effectively cleans hospital floor surfaces to a standard consistent with healthcare cleaning \nrequirements. \n(b) Supports autonomous operation with minimal operator intervention during routine cleaning activities. \n(c) Suitable for operation in public and non-clinical environments such as corridors, waiting areas, and \nadministrative spaces. \n\n \n \nPage | 7  \nOFFICIAL: Sensitive ? NSW Government \nOFFICIAL: Sensitive ? NSW Government \n(d) Describe available operating modes, including autonomous, manual, assisted, remote control, app-\nbased control, or other operator-controlled functions. \n(e) Describe the process for transitioning between autonomous and operator-controlled modes, including \nany operational limitations or disruptions. \n(f) \n Capability to rapidly respond to unplanned cleaning events, including spill response, spot cleaning, \nand urgent cleaning requests. \n(g)  Ability to prioritise, interrupt, or override scheduled cleaning tasks to address urgent operational \nrequirements. \n(h)  Describe operator intervention requirements during normal operation, exception handling, recovery \nfrom faults, and manual cleaning activities. \n7.2 \nAutonomous Capability \n(a) Navigation methods, such as use of sensors, LiDAR, cameras, or other technologies. \n(b) Mapping functionality, including initial setup and ongoing updates. \n(c) Obstacle detection and avoidance capabilities, including operation in dynamic environments with \npatients, staff, and visitors and glass walls. \n(d) Recovery and restart functionality following interruption or obstruction. \n(e) Define functionality constraints if operated in low connectivity or offline environments. \n7.3 \nReporting and Analytics \n(a) Cleaning performance reporting, including task completion and outcomes. \n(b) Coverage reporting, including mapped areas cleaned. \n(c) Usage reporting, including run times and utilisation rates. \n(d) Telemetry and operational dashboards are accessible to authorised users. \n(e) Audit capability, including logs of activities and system events. \n7.4 \nUsability and Workforce Integration \n(a) Ease of operation for cleaning staff across both autonomous and manual modes. \n(b) Training requirements for initial deployment and ongoing use. \n(c) User interface design and accessibility. \n(d) Staffing models required to support operations, including supervision and intervention requirements. \n7.5 \nReliability, Maintenance and Support \n(a) Typical uptime and operational availability, including service level commitments. \n(b) Preventative and corrective maintenance requirements. \n(c) Local support arrangements, including availability of service technicians within Australia. \n(d) Service desk operating hours and support coverage. \n(e) Response times for faults and breakdowns, including escalation arrangements. \n(f) \nIncident management processes and reporting. \n(g) Remote diagnostic and monitoring capability. \n(h) Spare parts availability and supply within Australia. \n(i) \nSoftware update and lifecycle management approach, including security update deployment. \n(j) \nSoftware maintenance windows and impact on service availability. \n\n \n \nPage | 8  \nOFFICIAL: Sensitive ? NSW Government \nOFFICIAL: Sensitive ? NSW Government \n(k) Expected asset life and recommended replacement cycle. \n7.6 \nSafety \n(a) Demonstration of appropriate safety controls for both autonomous and manual operation. \n(b) Compliance with relevant Australian safety standards and regulations. \n(c) Safe operation in environments with vulnerable persons, including patients and visitors. \n(d) Emergency stop functionality and incident management processes. \n7.7 \nInfection Prevention and Control (IPC) \n(a) Cleanability of the equipment, including requirements for cleaning equipment or self-clean functions \nand maintaining hygiene of brushes, tanks, filters, and other components. \n(b) Outline compatibility with hospital-grade disinfectants and cleaning chemicals the system utilises. \n(c) Any infection control risk assessments or validations undertaken. \n7.8 \nIntegration Capability.  \n(a) Available Application Programming Interfaces (APIs) and integration methods. \n(b) Authentication standards supported. \n(c) Data export capabilities and supported formats. \n(d) Real-time reporting and event notification capabilities. \n(e) Single Sign-On (SSO) support, including SAML, OAuth or OpenID Connect where available. \n(f) \nIntegration capabilities with enterprise reporting and facilities management systems. \n7.9 \nDigital Architecture, Cyber Security and Data Management Requirements \n7.9.1 \nGeneral Requirements \n(a) The Solution must comply with the NSW Cyber Security Policy and NSW Health Digital Health Cyber \nSecurity Policy, as applicable to the Solution and its intended use. \n(b) The Solution must be capable of operating securely within NSW Health environments and must \nsupport the applicable NSW Health cyber security, privacy, information governance, network and \ntechnology requirements. \n(c) The Supplier must provide information and supporting documentation reasonably required by NSW \nHealth, HealthShare NSW, eHealth NSW or their representatives to assess the Solution's digital \narchitecture, cyber security controls, data management practices and operational requirements. \n(d) The Supplier must participate in any applicable cyber security, privacy, architecture, technical or \ngovernance assessments required by NSW Health prior to implementation, trial or Contract award. \n7.9.2 \nData Governance and Management \n(a) The Solution must support clearly defined arrangements for data ownership, custody, access and \nadministrative control. \n(b) The Solution must include appropriate controls governing access to, management of and \nadministration of data. \n(c) The Supplier must identify all locations where data associated with the Solution is hosted, stored, \nprocessed or transmitted. \n(d) Where cloud services are used, the Supplier must identify the cloud services and hosting \narrangements applicable to the Solution. \n\n \n \nPage | 9  \nOFFICIAL: Sensitive ? NSW Government \nOFFICIAL: Sensitive ? NSW Government \n(e) The Supplier must identify whether any data associated with the Solution is stored, processed or \ntransmitted outside Australia. \n(f) \nThe Solution must support compliance with applicable privacy, health information and data protection \nlegislation, policies and regulatory obligations. \n7.9.3 \nSolution Architecture and Integration \n(a) The Supplier must maintain documented information describing the Solution Architecture, including: \ni. \nsystem components; \nii. \ninterfaces and integrations; \niii. data flows; \niv. network connectivity requirements; and \nv. \ndependencies on external systems or services. \n(b) The Solution must support integration with NSW Health digital, network and technology \nenvironments where integration is required. \n(c) The Solution must be capable of operating within restricted, segmented or limited-connectivity \nhealthcare network environments where required. \n(d) The Supplier must identify any external network, cloud service or third-party dependency required for \nthe normal operation of the Solution. \n7.9.4 \nCyber Security Controls \n(a) The Solution must implement industry-recognised cyber security controls appropriate to its risk \nprofile and intended use. \n(b) The Solution must support secure user authentication mechanisms. \n(c) The Solution must support role-based access controls (RBAC). \n(d) The Solution must support appropriate privileged access management controls. \n(e) The Solution must maintain auditable records of user and administrative activities. \n(f) \nThe Solution must support security monitoring and event logging appropriate to the Solution's risk \nprofile. \n(g) The Solution must provide sufficient logging and monitoring capability to support the detection, \ninvestigation and forensic review of security incidents. \n(h) The Supplier must maintain documented vulnerability management and security patching processes. \n(i) \nThe Supplier must have processes for notifying NSW Health, HealthShare NSW or their nominated \nrepresentative of identified security vulnerabilities and security incidents where required. \n(j) \nThe Supplier must ensure that remote access and vendor support activities are appropriately \nsecured, controlled and monitored. \n(k) Where available and applicable to the Solution, the Supplier must maintain relevant cyber security \ncertifications, independent assurance reports or equivalent security accreditations, including \ncertifications or frameworks such as ISO 27001 or NIST SP 800-82. \n7.9.5 \nBusiness Continuity and Operational Resilience \n(a) The Solution must include backup and recovery capabilities appropriate to the criticality of the \nservice. \n(b) The Solution must support documented business continuity and disaster recovery arrangements. \n(c) The Supplier must maintain documented system availability and support arrangements. \n\n \n \nPage | 10  \nOFFICIAL: Sensitive ? NSW Government \nOFFICIAL: Sensitive ? NSW Government \n(d) The Solution must continue to operate safely and predictably during technology failures or service \ndisruptions, including where applicable: \ni. \nloss of network connectivity; \nii. \ncloud service disruption; or \niii. disruption to connected systems or services. \n7.9.6 \nPrivacy and Information Protection \n(a) The Supplier must identify the types of information that may be collected, stored, processed or \ntransmitted by the Solution. \n(b) The Solution must identify and control any capability that may intentionally or unintentionally capture \ninformation relating to patients, staff or visitors. \n(c) The Solution must implement appropriate privacy controls, including where applicable: \ni. \ndata minimisation; \nii. \ndata masking; \niii. de-identification; and \niv. access restrictions. \n(d) The Solution must restrict access to information based on authorised user roles and operational \nrequirements. \n(e) The Solution must include measures to support compliance with applicable privacy, health \ninformation and data protection obligations. \n7.9.7 \nSensors, Imaging, Audio and Tracking Capabilities \n(a) The Supplier must identify and document all Solution capabilities that collect, capture, record or \nmonitor information, including: \ni. \nsensors; \nii. \ncameras; \niii. image capture or recording; \niv. audio recording; \nv. \nlocation tracking; and \nvi. other monitoring, recording or data collection functions. \n(b) The Supplier must identify the purpose and use of any such capability and the type of information \nthat may be collected or generated. \n7.9.8 \nAdditional Requirements for Autonomous, Robotic, OT and IoT Solutions \n(a) Where the Solution incorporates autonomous, robotic, Operational Technology (OT) or Internet of \nThings (IoT) capabilities, the Solution must: \ni. \nimplement controls to prevent unauthorised access, manipulation, modification or compromise of \nthe device; \nii. \noperate in a safe and predictable state during loss of network connectivity; \niii. operate in a safe and predictable state during cloud service disruption; \niv. operate in a safe and predictable state during cyber security incidents affecting connected \nsystems; \nv. \ninclude safety mechanisms designed to protect patients, visitors, staff and healthcare operations \nin the event of malfunction, system failure or cyber security compromise; and \n\n \n \nPage | 11  \nOFFICIAL: Sensitive ? NSW Government \nOFFICIAL: Sensitive ? NSW Government \nvi. identify and manage dependencies on external networks, cloud services and third-party providers \nrequired for normal operation. \n(b) The Solution must be designed so that a cyber security event or loss of connectivity does not result in \nan unsafe operating condition for patients, visitors, staff or healthcare operations. \n7.9.9 \nAssurance and Assessment Requirements \n(a) The Supplier must, when requested by NSW Health, HealthShare NSW, eHealth NSW or their \nrepresentatives, participate in cyber security, privacy, architecture, technical and governance \nassurance activities. \n(b) Assurance activities may include, but are not limited to: \ni. \nSupplier Security Assessment Questionnaire (SSAQ) assessments; \nii. \narchitecture and technical assurance reviews; \niii. privacy and information governance assessments; \niv. network and infrastructure assessments; and \n(c) NSW Health and eHealth NSW assurance and deployment processes are required for \nimplementation within a healthcare environment. \n(d) The Supplier must provide reasonable information, documentation, and access necessary to support \nthese assurance activities. \n(e) Completion of applicable assurance activities may be required before the Solution progresses to a \ntrial, implementation, deployment or Contract award, as determined by NSW Health or HealthShare \nNSW. \n \n7.10 \nOperational Characteristics \n(a) Suppliers should declare key operational specifications, including: \ni. \nNoise levels during operation. \nii. \nBattery type, charging approach, and operational runtime. \niii. \nCharging or battery management strategy to support continuous operation. \niv. \nTank capacity, where applicable, and coverage per fill. \nNote: Information on dirt detection sensors may be provided where available but are not mandatory. \n7.11 \nCommercial and Lifecycle Information \n(a) Suppliers should provide indicative commercial information, including: \ni. \nCapital purchase cost for equipment. \nii. \nLeasing or as-a-service options, including commercial structure. \niii. \nBattery replacement costs and lifecycle. \niv. \nConsumable costs, including brushes, filters, and cleaning agents. \nv. \nExpected asset life and depreciation considerations. \nvi. \nEnd-of-life disposal or recycling arrangements. \nvii. \nWarranty terms and conditions. \nviii. \nAny implementation or onboarding costs. \nNote: HSNSW intends to undertake a proof-of-concept trial as phase 2 to this procurement to assess the \noperational suitability of proposed solutions within a live healthcare environment. Equipment selected for \n\n \n \nPage | 12  \nOFFICIAL: Sensitive ? NSW Government \nOFFICIAL: Sensitive ? NSW Government \nparticipation in the trial may be subject to direct purchase for the purposes of evaluation and deployment \nacross multiple sites during this period. \nSuppliers should ensure that pricing provided for capital purchase is clear, complete, and reflective of all \ncomponents required for operational use. \nIn addition, suppliers are requested to provide detailed information on alternative commercial models, \nincluding leasing, rental, or as-a-service arrangements. This information will inform potential future \nprocurement approaches beyond the trial phase, including scalability, whole-of-life cost optimisation, and \nalignment with operational funding models. \nHSNSW is seeking flexibility in commercial structures to support both initial evaluation and longer-term \nadoption, subject to performance, value for money, and organisational requirements. \n \n8. \nEOI Instructions and Key Information \n8.1 \nAuthorised Contact Officer \nYou may contact the following person if you have any questions or require clarification on any topics \ncovered in this EOI: \nAuthorised Contact Officer \nName \nMurray Affleck \nPhone No. \n02 8644 2528 \nEmail \nMurray.Affleck@health.nsw.gov.au \n8.2 \nClosing Date and Time \nAll responses to this EOI must be submitted via the NSW Government buy NSW website (detailed below) \nby the nominated closing date and time. \nLodgement Details \nWebsite \nhttps://buy.nsw.gov.au/opportunity \nClosing Date \n25 September 2026 \nClosing Time \n3:00 pm (Australian Eastern Standard Time or Australian daylight-saving time, \nas applicable) \n8.3 \nDocument Standards \nElectronic submissions must meet the following document standards: \n? \nResponse Schedules: Microsoft Word (.doc or .docx) or PDF (.pdf) \n? \nSupporting Documents: Microsoft Word (.doc or .docx), PDF (.pdf), Microsoft Presentation (.ppt or \n.pptx) or Microsoft Excel (.xls or .xlsx) \n \n \n\n \n \nPage | 13  \nOFFICIAL: Sensitive ? NSW Government \nOFFICIAL: Sensitive ? NSW Government \n8.4 \nProposed Timeframe \nProvisional Timetable \nEOI issued \n9 September 2026 \nEnd period for questions or requests for information \n16 September 2026 \nEOI closes \n25 September 2026 \nEvaluation of submissions \n28 Sep ? 16 Oct 2026 \nICT and Digital Security assessment \nOct ? Nov 2026 \nTarget date for next stage ? Trial Start Date \nFebruary 2027 \nTarget Date for Phae 3 Request for Tender \n2 July 2027 \nThe above timeline is provisional only. The Agency may, at its discretion, amend the provisional EOI \nprogram. \n9. \nEvaluation Criteria \nSelection will follow the Evaluation process as set out Part A - Section 13.1. \nThe evaluation criteria that will be used to evaluate the Respondents are specified below: \n9.1 \nNon-Price Criteria \nNon-Price Criteria \n1. Solution Overview \nAssessment will consider: \n? Clarity and completeness of the proposed solution \n? Suitability of technology type for healthcare environments \n? Alignment of proposed models to requirements \n? Demonstrated understanding of intended use \n2. Functional Capability and Operational Performance \nAssessment will consider: \n? Ability to achieve healthcare cleaning standards across hospital floor surfaces \n? Suitability for operation in public and non-clinical healthcare environments \n? Effectiveness in delivering consistent cleaning outcomes across routine and ad hoc scenarios \n? Capability to respond to unplanned cleaning events, including spill response and urgent cleaning needs \n? Overall performance capability, including coverage, reliability, and operational effectiveness \n3. Autonomous Capability  \nAssessment will consider: \n? Navigation and mapping capability \n? Obstacle detection and avoidance \n? Reliability of autonomous operation \n\n \n \nPage | 14  \nOFFICIAL: Sensitive ? NSW Government \nOFFICIAL: Sensitive ? NSW Government \n? Ability to operate in low or no connectivity environments \n4. Reporting and Analytics \nAssessment will consider: \n? Quality and usability of reporting outputs \n? Availability of performance and usage data \n? Dashboard functionality and insights \n? Auditability and traceability of activities \n5. Usability and Workforce Integration  \nAssessment will consider: \n? Ease of use for operators \n? Training requirements and complexity \n? Integration with existing workforce and workflows \n? Change management considerations \n6. Reliability, Maintenance and Support \nAssessment will consider: \n? Expected uptime and reliability \n? Maintenance requirements and servicing model \n? Availability of local support in Australia \n? Spare parts availability and lifecycle support \n7. Safety \nAssessment will consider: \n? Safety features and controls \n? Compliance with Australian standards \n? Suitability for use around patients, visitors, and staff \n? Incident response and emergency functionality \n8. Infection Prevention and Control (IPC) \nAssessment will consider: \n? Cleanability of equipment \n? Compatibility with healthcare cleaning agents \n? Controls to prevent cross-contamination \n? Evidence of IPC validation or assessment \n9. Cyber Security and Data Management \nAssessment will consider: \n? Data ownership, custody, access and governance \n? Data hosting, processing and transmission locations, including cloud architecture \n? Solution architecture, integrations, data flows and external dependencies \n? Cyber security controls, certifications and independent assurance \n? Authentication, role-based access control and privileged access management \n? Logging, monitoring, audit and security event capabilities \n\n \n \nPage | 15  \nOFFICIAL: Sensitive ? NSW Government \nOFFICIAL: Sensitive ? NSW Government \n? Privacy and information protection controls, including the handling of images, audio, location and other \ncaptured information \n? Backup, recovery, disaster recovery and business continuity arrangements \n10. Operational Characteristics \nAssessment will consider: \n? Noise levels and suitability for hospital use \n? Battery performance and runtime \n? Charging requirements and efficiency \n? Tank capacity and operational efficiency \n11. Commercial and Lifecycle Information \nAssessment will consider: \n? Overall cost structure and value for money \n? Transparency of lifecycle costs \n? Flexibility of commercial models \n? Warranty and end-of-life considerations \n12. Proof of Concept Trial \nAssessment will consider: \n? Ease of deployment and relocation \n? Lead times for supply \n? Practicality of trial implementation \n? Supplier support during trial phase \n10. \nTerms and Conditions of Participation \n10.1 \nNot an Offer \nThis EOI is not an offer. It is a formal request for Respondents to submit a response demonstrating their \neligibility or capability to meet the organisation?s qualitative criteria for the supply of goods and/or \nservices, as detailed in this Part A document. Nothing in this EOI should be interpreted as creating a \nbinding contract (express or implied) between the Agency and any Respondents for the supply of goods \nand/or services. \n10.2 \nNon-Disclosure Agreement \nRespondents selected to participate in the RFT Phase 2 will be required to enter into a Non-Disclosure \nAgreement with the Agency to ensure that all confidential information shared during the RFT process is \nkept confidential. \n10.3 \nOrganisation Rights \nNotwithstanding anything else in this EOI, and without limiting its rights at law or otherwise, the Agency \nreserves the right, in its absolute discretion at any time, to: \n(a) vary or extend any time or date specified in this Invitation for all or any Respondents; or \n(b) terminate the participation of any Respondent or any other person in the EOI process. \n10.4 \nFormat and content \nRespondents must ensure that the Response uses the format and addresses the content requirements set \nout in the EOI Response documents. \n\n \n \nPage | 16  \nOFFICIAL: Sensitive ? NSW Government \nOFFICIAL: Sensitive ? NSW Government \nWord or page limits (where specified) should be observed. The Agency may, at its discretion, disregard any \nparts of the Response that exceed any specified word or page limits. \nResponses and other information provided in the EOI must be submitted in writing and in English. \n10.5 \nAmendments to this EOI \nThe Agency may, at its discretion, amend this EOI by giving appropriate written notice of an amendment. \nIf the Agency varies or supplements this EOI, it will issue an Addendum and Reference Material (if \nrequired) on the NSW Government buy NSW website https://buy.nsw.gov.au/suppliers. In each case, an \nAddendum or Reference Material becomes part of this EOI. \nIt is the obligation of the Respondent to check if any Addenda or Reference Material were issued prior to \nthe Closing Date and Time, even if a Response has already been submitted. \nRespondents must check the buy NSW website and download all Addenda and Reference Material. \n11. \nLegal entity \nResponses must be submitted by a legal entity or, if a joint tender, consortium or otherwise, by legal \nentities with the capacity to contract. The Agency will only enter into any proposed Contract with such \nlegal entity or entities. \nAny Response which is submitted by a combination of two or more organisations, as a joint venture, a \nconsortium or otherwise, must include in the Response: \n(a) information for each member of the group. \n(b) a clear statement of the nature of the association between the members of the group and the basis \nupon which they propose to contract with the Agency; and \n(c) a statement that each member of the group is jointly and severally liable for the performance of the \nother members of the group under the resultant contract or that one member of the group is fully \nliable for the performance of all members of the group. \nThe Agency may ask a Respondent to provide evidence of its legal status or capacity to contract. If \nResponses from entities propose to contract in their capacity as trustees, such evidence may include \ncopies of the relevant trust deeds. Any evidence requested is to be provided within three (3) working days \nof the request. \n12. \nDiscontinuance of EOI Process \nWithout limiting its other rights under this EOI or at law or otherwise, the Agency reserves the right to \ndiscontinue the EOI process at any point, without making a determination regarding acceptance or \nrejection of Responses. The Agency will advise Respondents to this effect. \n13. \nEthics \nRespondents are to comply with the NSW Health Code of Conduct  \nhttps://www1.health.nsw.gov.au/pds/ActivePDSDocuments/PD2015_049.pdf \nand the NSW Government?s Procurement Policy Framework \nhttps://buy.nsw.gov.au/policy-library/policies/procurement-policy-framework \n \n \n \n\n \n \nPage | 17  \nOFFICIAL: Sensitive ? NSW Government \nOFFICIAL: Sensitive ? NSW Government \n14. \nLodgement of Responses \n14.1 \nValidity Period \nThe Respondent?s submission is to be valid throughout the EOI evaluation process from the lodgement \nclosing date. \nRespondents are to note that its submission to this EOI is made at its own cost, and that the Agency whilst \nmaking the request in good faith, is not obliged to proceed with this project. \n14.2 \nElectronic Lodgement of Responses \nRespondents should not wait until the nominated closing time to lodge their responses as difficulties in \nuploading responses to the website may be encountered where many users attempt to upload responses \nat the same time or while attempting to upload large file sizes or volumes. \nA response lodged wholly or partly after the Closing time will be registered as a Late Response and may \nbe excluded from evaluation. The Agency may, at its discretion, allow the evaluation of a Late Response, if \nit judges that the lateness is due to circumstances outside the control of the Respondent. \nBy lodging an EOI, the Respondent agrees that the EOI remains valid and open for acceptance by the \nAgency for the Tender Validity Period.  \n14.3 \nCommunications \nAny requests for clarification in relation to this EOI or the EOI Process must be: \n? \ncommunicated to the Contact Officer in writing; and \n? \nsubmitted by the last date for questions set out in section 6.4 or asked during any briefing session. \nThe Agency is not obliged to respond to any question or request. \nIn all cases the Agency reserves the right to forward the question and details of the Agency's response to \nall Tenderers on a non-attributable basis. However, the Agency will give the Tenderer the opportunity to \nwithdraw the question where the Tenderer has requested that the question not be disclosed. \nApart from the processes identified in these Conditions, Respondents must not contact any \nRepresentative of the Agency, NSW Health or the NSW Government (including any members of \nparliament or their advisers) to discuss any aspect of the EOI or future RFP process, except as authorised \nin writing by the Agency. \nResponses and other information provided in the EOI must be submitted in writing and in English. \n14.4 \nLate Responses \nLate Responses may not be considered, except where the Agency is satisfied that the integrity and \ncompetitiveness of the EOI process has not been compromised. The Agency will not penalise any \nRespondent whose response is received late, if the delay is due solely to mishandling by the Agency. \n14.5 \nExtension of Closing Date and Time \nThe Agency may, at its discretion, extend the Closing Date and Time. \n15. \nEvaluation \n15.1 \nEvaluation Process \n(a) The Agency will evaluate Expressions of Interest (EOIs) based on the criteria detailed in section B of \nthis EOI document. This evaluation aims to determine the suitability of potential suppliers for the next \nstage of the procurement process. The information provided by Respondents in their EOI Responses \nwill be used to assess how well they meet each criterion. \n\n \n \nPage | 18  \nOFFICIAL: Sensitive ? NSW Government \nOFFICIAL: Sensitive ? NSW Government \n(b) Respondents are strongly advised to provide clear and comprehensive responses to all evaluation \ncriteria outlined in this EOI document. This will help ensure that their submissions are properly \nassessed. \n(c) The Agency may exercise discretion in evaluating EOIs and managing the EOI Process. This includes \nthe evaluation of any subjective criteria where applicable. By submitting an EOI, Respondents \nacknowledge that the Agency?s decision on their EOI and those of other Respondents will be final and \nbinding. \n(d) The Agency may consider alternative proposals submitted alongside a conforming EOI. Such \nalternatives will be assessed based on their merit and alignment with the overall objectives of the \nprocurement. \n15.2 \nAcceptance or Rejection of EOIs \nThe Agency is not obligated to accept any EOI received. The decision to accept or reject EOIs is at the \nAgency?s sole discretion. \nThe Agency reserves the right to: \n(a) Accept or reject any EOI based on its alignment with the evaluation criteria and the Agency?s specific \nneeds and requirements. \n(b) Reject EOIs that do not meet the required format or content, or that lack sufficient information to \nenable a thorough evaluation. \n(c) Reject any non-conforming EOIs, including those that fail to meet mandatory criteria or do not offer a \nviable basis for further consideration. This includes EOIs where the proposed solution or pricing is \ndeemed excessive or unrealistic. \n(d) Request additional information or clarifications from Respondents to better understand their \nproposals or to address any ambiguities. \n(e) Modify the EOI process or timelines as needed to ensure the best outcome for the procurement. \n(f) \nConduct due diligence on the Respondent?s background, including commercial, business, and credit \nhistory, to assess their capability and reliability. \n(g) Invite shortlisted Respondents to provide further details or participate in discussions or presentations \nto elaborate on their EOI. \n(h) Reject all EOIs and terminate the EOI Process at any stage before issuing a Request for Proposal \n(RFP). If necessary, a new EOI process may be initiated with revised criteria to better meet the \nAgency?s needs. \n15.3 \nRequest for Clarification by the Agency \nAfter the Closing Date and Time and before moving to the RFP stage, the Agency may request \nclarifications from Respondents in writing. This is to ensure that all submitted EOIs are fully understood \nand evaluated accurately. \nRespondents must: \n(a) Respond to any requests for clarification within the specified timeframe and in the format requested \nby the Agency. \n(b) Ensure that their responses to clarification requests address the Agency?s queries comprehensively \nand comply with the requirements set forth in the EOI document. \n(c) Not use requests for clarification as an opportunity to enhance their EOI or gain an advantage over \nother Respondents. All clarifications should be factual and directly relevant to the Agency?s requests. \n \n\n"}, {'file_name': 'HSNSW_6108---EOI-Part-A---Terms-Conditions-and-Requirements.txt', 'extracted_text': " \n \nOFFICIAL: Sensitive ? NSW Government \nOFFICIAL: Sensitive ? NSW Government \n \n \nHealthShare NSW \nHealthShare NSW invites this Expression of Interest for and on behalf of \nNSW Health Administration Corporation (HAC) \n \n \n \n \n \n \n  \nExpression of Interest (EOI) \nPart A - Terms & Conditions & Requirements \n  \n \nNo. \nHSNSW_6108 \nName \nCleaning Automation Initiative \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \nwww.health.nsw.gov.au \n\n \nPage | 2  \nOFFICIAL: Sensitive ? NSW Government \nOFFICIAL: Sensitive ? NSW Government \nCONTENTS \nSection A \nEOI Terms and Conditions \n3 \n1. \nIntroduction \n3 \n2. \nContext \n3 \n3. \nProcurement Process \n4 \n4. \nEOI Structure \n5 \n5. \nRequirements Overview \n6 \n6. \nGoods and Services Sought \n6 \n7. \nStatement of Requirements \n6 \n8. \nEOI Instructions and Key Information \n12 \n9. \nEvaluation Criteria \n13 \n10. \nTerms and Conditions of Participation \n15 \n11. \nLegal entity \n16 \n12. \nDiscontinuance of EOI Process \n16 \n13. \nEthics \n16 \n14. \nLodgement of Responses \n17 \n15. \nEvaluation \n17 \n\n \nPage | 3  \nOFFICIAL: Sensitive ? NSW Government \nOFFICIAL: Sensitive ? NSW Government \nSection A \nEOI Terms and Conditions \n1. \nIntroduction \n1.1 \nAgency Details \nThis Expression of Interest (EOI) is issued by HealthShare NSW on behalf of the Agency \nAgency details \n \nAgency name \nHealthShare NSW \nAgency address \n1 Reserve Road, St Leonards NSW 2065 \nHealthShare NSW (HSNSW) is a unit within the Health Administration Corporation and provides shared \nservices, including shared procurement services, to NSW Health agencies. HSNSW is responsible for the \nconduct of this EOI, assisted by the Agency. References to the ?Agency? will encompass references to the \nHealth Administration Corporation, HSNSW and NSW Health as appropriate. \n2. \nContext \n2.1 \nBackground \nHSNSW provides environmental cleaning services across a large and diverse portfolio of public health \nfacilities throughout New South Wales. \nIn 2023, HSNSW undertook a trial of autonomous floor scrubbing technology within a hospital \nenvironment to evaluate the potential benefits of cleaning automation. Outcomes from this trial identified \nopportunities to improve cleaning consistency, enhance workforce utilisation, reduce repetitive manual \nhandling activities, and improve visibility of cleaning activities through digital reporting and telemetry. \nSince completion of the trial, autonomous cleaning technologies have continued to mature considerably. \nHSNSW is now seeking to assess a broader range of cleaning automation solutions, including autonomous \nfloor scrubbers, autonomous vacuum cleaners. \nThis Expression of Interest forms the first stage of a three-stage procurement process intended to: \n? \nUnderstand current market capability. \n? \nAssess the maturity of available technologies. \n? \nEvaluate suitability for healthcare environments. \n? \nIdentify suppliers for participation in a structured trial program. \n? \nInform future procurement and implementation decisions. \n2.2 \nOverall Project Objectives \nEvaluate Cleaning Automation Technologies \nAssess the effectiveness, usability, safety, reliability, and operational suitability of autonomous cleaning \ntechnologies in healthcare environments. \nSupport Workforce Sustainability \nInvestigate how automation may support existing cleaning services in non-clinical areas by reducing \nrepetitive manual tasks. It also supports staff to perform environmental cleaning in higher-risk clinical \nareas, ensuring compliance with the minimum cleaning standards outlined in the Policy Directive \nPD2023_018. \n \nImprove Operational Efficiency \n\n \n \nPage | 4  \nOFFICIAL: Sensitive ? NSW Government \nOFFICIAL: Sensitive ? NSW Government \nAssess opportunities to improve cleaning consistency, auditability, productivity, and service \nresponsiveness. \nSupport Innovation and Future Readiness \nIdentify technologies that may support future NSW Health service delivery models and hospital \ninfrastructure planning. \nInform Future Procurement \nGenerate evidence-based findings to support future procurement decisions relating to cleaning \nautomation technologies. \n \n3. \nProcurement Process \n3.1 \nOverview \nThe procurement process will follow a multi-stage approach: \n? \nPhase 1: Expression of Interest (EOI) \n? \nPhase 2: Placement Trials \n? \nPhase 3: Request for Tender (RFT) \n \n3.2 \nPhase 1 ? The EOI \nThe purpose of the EOI is to identify suppliers with suitable autonomous cleaning technologies and \ncapability to participate in a trial program. \nSuppliers may respond to one or more of the following technology categories: \n? \nAutonomous floor scrubbers \n? \nAutonomous vacuum cleaners \nResponses will be assessed against the evaluation criteria detailed in this EOI to establish a shortlist of \nsuppliers for Phase 2.  \n3.3 \nPhase 2 ? Placement Trials \nHSNSW intends to undertake a structured 4-month trial program involving selected suppliers and \ntechnologies at multiple NSW Health facilities as identified below in section 4.1. \nIt is anticipated if selected equipment will undertake trials for 2 months at each location across two \nselected locations. \nThe purpose of the trial program is to evaluate: \n? \nOperational suitability \n? \nCleaning performance \n? \nSafety \n? \nReliability \n? \nStaff usability \n? \nMaintenance requirements \n? \nReporting capability \n? \nScalability across healthcare environments \nFindings from the trial program will inform future procurement and supplier panel decisions. \n \n \n\n \n \nPage | 5  \nOFFICIAL: Sensitive ? NSW Government \nOFFICIAL: Sensitive ? NSW Government \n3.4 \nPhase 3 -Request for Tender (RFT) \nSubject to successful completion of the trial phase and approval to proceed, HSNSW may conduct a \nlimited tender process to establish a supplier panel for cleaning automation technologies. \nThe RFT will include detailed specifications, pricing schedules, and contractual terms and conditions. \nOnly suppliers shortlisted from the EOI phase will be eligible to participate in this subsequent stage. \n4. \nEOI Structure \nThis EOI comprises the following documents: \n? \nPart A Terms & Conditions and Requirements \n? \nPart B Response Schedules \nEOI responses must contain the completed Schedules which are included in the document ?Part B \nResponse Schedules?.  \nResponses should focus on demonstrating organisational capability, relevant experience, technical \nmaturity, implementation approach, and suitability of proposed solutions for healthcare environments. \nNote that any variation from the prescribed format in the respondent?s compulsory compliant response \nmay result in the Response being excluded from evaluation. \n4.1 \nScope of the EOI \nThe purpose of this EOI is to identify and assess suitably qualified suppliers capable of providing \nautonomous cleaning technologies suitable for deployment within NSW Health facilities. \nHSNSW intends to evaluate a range of autonomous cleaning solutions to support future decision-making \nregarding potential implementation across healthcare environments. \nSuppliers may respond for one or more of the following technology categories: \n? \nAutonomous Floor Scrubbers: Autonomous or semi-autonomous floor cleaning systems capable of \nperforming routine floor scrubbing activities within healthcare facilities. \n? \nAutonomous Vacuum Cleaners: Autonomous or semi-autonomous vacuum-cleaning systems suitable \nfor commercial and healthcare environments. \nThrough this EOI, HSNSW seeks to: \n? \nUnderstand the current market capability and available technologies. \n? \nIdentify suppliers with demonstrated experience delivering autonomous cleaning solutions. \n? \nAssess the maturity and reliability of available systems. \n? \nUnderstand deployment, support and maintenance requirements. \n? \nAssess suitability for healthcare environments. \n? \nExplore commercial delivery models, including purchase & leasing arrangements. \n? \nIdentify suppliers suitable for participation in a multi-site trial program.  \n \nProposed sites are; \no Westmead Hospital,  \no Royal North Shore Hospital, \no Tamworth Hospital,  \no Wagga Wagga Base Hospital, \no Southeast Regional Hospital (Bega),  \no Nepean Hospital. \n \n\n \n \nPage | 6  \nOFFICIAL: Sensitive ? NSW Government \nOFFICIAL: Sensitive ? NSW Government \nResponses should focus on demonstrating capability and suitability rather than providing detailed pricing \nsubmissions. \n \n4.2 \nExpected Contract & Duration of RFT \nStanding Offer Agreement \nThe Agency proposes to enter into the proposed Agreement with the successful tenderers to establish a \npanel of suppliers for the Goods and Services.  \nIt is envisaged that the panel will be in place for a term of three (3) years, which may be extended for two \n(2) x twelve (12) month options.  \n \n5. \nRequirements Overview \n5.1 \nHSNSW is seeking information from suppliers capable of providing autonomous and operator-assisted \ncleaning technologies suitable for healthcare environments. The purpose of this request is to assess \nmarket capability, operational suitability, and alignment with NSW Health requirements, including safety, \ninfection control, workforce integration, and digital security. \n5.2 \nSuppliers should describe available operating modes, including autonomous, supervised, assisted, manual, \nremote-control, or other intervention capabilities where available. \n6. \nGoods and Services Sought \n6.1 \nSuppliers may respond for one or more technology categories, including: \n6.1.1 \nAutonomous Floor Scrubbers \n(a) Solutions capable of performing autonomous and operator-assisted floor scrubbing and wet cleaning \nactivities within hospital and healthcare environments. \n6.1.2 \nAutonomous Vacuum Cleaners \n(a) Solutions capable of performing autonomous and operator-assisted dry vacuuming and floor \nmaintenance activities within hospital and healthcare environments. \n7. \nStatement of Requirements \nHSNSW seeks cleaning technologies capable of operating safely, effectively, and reliably within complex \nhealthcare environments, including public and non-clinical areas. Solutions must support both routine \ncleaning and responsive cleaning activities when required. \nSuppliers must describe how their solution meets the following capability areas. \n7.1 \nFunctional Capability and Operational Control \n(a) Effectively cleans hospital floor surfaces to a standard consistent with healthcare cleaning \nrequirements. \n(b) Supports autonomous operation with minimal operator intervention during routine cleaning activities. \n(c) Suitable for operation in public and non-clinical environments such as corridors, waiting areas, and \nadministrative spaces. \n\n \n \nPage | 7  \nOFFICIAL: Sensitive ? NSW Government \nOFFICIAL: Sensitive ? NSW Government \n(d) Describe available operating modes, including autonomous, manual, assisted, remote control, app-\nbased control, or other operator-controlled functions. \n(e) Describe the process for transitioning between autonomous and operator-controlled modes, including \nany operational limitations or disruptions. \n(f) \n Capability to rapidly respond to unplanned cleaning events, including spill response, spot cleaning, \nand urgent cleaning requests. \n(g)  Ability to prioritise, interrupt, or override scheduled cleaning tasks to address urgent operational \nrequirements. \n(h)  Describe operator intervention requirements during normal operation, exception handling, recovery \nfrom faults, and manual cleaning activities. \n7.2 \nAutonomous Capability \n(a) Navigation methods, such as use of sensors, LiDAR, cameras, or other technologies. \n(b) Mapping functionality, including initial setup and ongoing updates. \n(c) Obstacle detection and avoidance capabilities, including operation in dynamic environments with \npatients, staff, and visitors and glass walls. \n(d) Recovery and restart functionality following interruption or obstruction. \n(e) Define functionality constraints if operated in low connectivity or offline environments. \n7.3 \nReporting and Analytics \n(a) Cleaning performance reporting, including task completion and outcomes. \n(b) Coverage reporting, including mapped areas cleaned. \n(c) Usage reporting, including run times and utilisation rates. \n(d) Telemetry and operational dashboards are accessible to authorised users. \n(e) Audit capability, including logs of activities and system events. \n7.4 \nUsability and Workforce Integration \n(a) Ease of operation for cleaning staff across both autonomous and manual modes. \n(b) Training requirements for initial deployment and ongoing use. \n(c) User interface design and accessibility. \n(d) Staffing models required to support operations, including supervision and intervention requirements. \n7.5 \nReliability, Maintenance and Support \n(a) Typical uptime and operational availability, including service level commitments. \n(b) Preventative and corrective maintenance requirements. \n(c) Local support arrangements, including availability of service technicians within Australia. \n(d) Service desk operating hours and support coverage. \n(e) Response times for faults and breakdowns, including escalation arrangements. \n(f) \nIncident management processes and reporting. \n(g) Remote diagnostic and monitoring capability. \n(h) Spare parts availability and supply within Australia. \n(i) \nSoftware update and lifecycle management approach, including security update deployment. \n(j) \nSoftware maintenance windows and impact on service availability. \n\n \n \nPage | 8  \nOFFICIAL: Sensitive ? NSW Government \nOFFICIAL: Sensitive ? NSW Government \n(k) Expected asset life and recommended replacement cycle. \n7.6 \nSafety \n(a) Demonstration of appropriate safety controls for both autonomous and manual operation. \n(b) Compliance with relevant Australian safety standards and regulations. \n(c) Safe operation in environments with vulnerable persons, including patients and visitors. \n(d) Emergency stop functionality and incident management processes. \n7.7 \nInfection Prevention and Control (IPC) \n(a) Cleanability of the equipment, including requirements for cleaning equipment or self-clean functions \nand maintaining hygiene of brushes, tanks, filters, and other components. \n(b) Outline compatibility with hospital-grade disinfectants and cleaning chemicals the system utilises. \n(c) Any infection control risk assessments or validations undertaken. \n7.8 \nIntegration Capability.  \n(a) Available Application Programming Interfaces (APIs) and integration methods. \n(b) Authentication standards supported. \n(c) Data export capabilities and supported formats. \n(d) Real-time reporting and event notification capabilities. \n(e) Single Sign-On (SSO) support, including SAML, OAuth or OpenID Connect where available. \n(f) \nIntegration capabilities with enterprise reporting and facilities management systems. \n7.9 \nDigital Architecture, Cyber Security and Data Management Requirements \n7.9.1 \nGeneral Requirements \n(a) The Solution must comply with the NSW Cyber Security Policy and NSW Health Digital Health Cyber \nSecurity Policy, as applicable to the Solution and its intended use. \n(b) The Solution must be capable of operating securely within NSW Health environments and must \nsupport the applicable NSW Health cyber security, privacy, information governance, network and \ntechnology requirements. \n(c) The Supplier must provide information and supporting documentation reasonably required by NSW \nHealth, HealthShare NSW, eHealth NSW or their representatives to assess the Solution's digital \narchitecture, cyber security controls, data management practices and operational requirements. \n(d) The Supplier must participate in any applicable cyber security, privacy, architecture, technical or \ngovernance assessments required by NSW Health prior to implementation, trial or Contract award. \n7.9.2 \nData Governance and Management \n(a) The Solution must support clearly defined arrangements for data ownership, custody, access and \nadministrative control. \n(b) The Solution must include appropriate controls governing access to, management of and \nadministration of data. \n(c) The Supplier must identify all locations where data associated with the Solution is hosted, stored, \nprocessed or transmitted. \n(d) Where cloud services are used, the Supplier must identify the cloud services and hosting \narrangements applicable to the Solution. \n\n \n \nPage | 9  \nOFFICIAL: Sensitive ? NSW Government \nOFFICIAL: Sensitive ? NSW Government \n(e) The Supplier must identify whether any data associated with the Solution is stored, processed or \ntransmitted outside Australia. \n(f) \nThe Solution must support compliance with applicable privacy, health information and data protection \nlegislation, policies and regulatory obligations. \n7.9.3 \nSolution Architecture and Integration \n(a) The Supplier must maintain documented information describing the Solution Architecture, including: \ni. \nsystem components; \nii. \ninterfaces and integrations; \niii. data flows; \niv. network connectivity requirements; and \nv. \ndependencies on external systems or services. \n(b) The Solution must support integration with NSW Health digital, network and technology \nenvironments where integration is required. \n(c) The Solution must be capable of operating within restricted, segmented or limited-connectivity \nhealthcare network environments where required. \n(d) The Supplier must identify any external network, cloud service or third-party dependency required for \nthe normal operation of the Solution. \n7.9.4 \nCyber Security Controls \n(a) The Solution must implement industry-recognised cyber security controls appropriate to its risk \nprofile and intended use. \n(b) The Solution must support secure user authentication mechanisms. \n(c) The Solution must support role-based access controls (RBAC). \n(d) The Solution must support appropriate privileged access management controls. \n(e) The Solution must maintain auditable records of user and administrative activities. \n(f) \nThe Solution must support security monitoring and event logging appropriate to the Solution's risk \nprofile. \n(g) The Solution must provide sufficient logging and monitoring capability to support the detection, \ninvestigation and forensic review of security incidents. \n(h) The Supplier must maintain documented vulnerability management and security patching processes. \n(i) \nThe Supplier must have processes for notifying NSW Health, HealthShare NSW or their nominated \nrepresentative of identified security vulnerabilities and security incidents where required. \n(j) \nThe Supplier must ensure that remote access and vendor support activities are appropriately \nsecured, controlled and monitored. \n(k) Where available and applicable to the Solution, the Supplier must maintain relevant cyber security \ncertifications, independent assurance reports or equivalent security accreditations, including \ncertifications or frameworks such as ISO 27001 or NIST SP 800-82. \n7.9.5 \nBusiness Continuity and Operational Resilience \n(a) The Solution must include backup and recovery capabilities appropriate to the criticality of the \nservice. \n(b) The Solution must support documented business continuity and disaster recovery arrangements. \n(c) The Supplier must maintain documented system availability and support arrangements. \n\n \n \nPage | 10  \nOFFICIAL: Sensitive ? NSW Government \nOFFICIAL: Sensitive ? NSW Government \n(d) The Solution must continue to operate safely and predictably during technology failures or service \ndisruptions, including where applicable: \ni. \nloss of network connectivity; \nii. \ncloud service disruption; or \niii. disruption to connected systems or services. \n7.9.6 \nPrivacy and Information Protection \n(a) The Supplier must identify the types of information that may be collected, stored, processed or \ntransmitted by the Solution. \n(b) The Solution must identify and control any capability that may intentionally or unintentionally capture \ninformation relating to patients, staff or visitors. \n(c) The Solution must implement appropriate privacy controls, including where applicable: \ni. \ndata minimisation; \nii. \ndata masking; \niii. de-identification; and \niv. access restrictions. \n(d) The Solution must restrict access to information based on authorised user roles and operational \nrequirements. \n(e) The Solution must include measures to support compliance with applicable privacy, health \ninformation and data protection obligations. \n7.9.7 \nSensors, Imaging, Audio and Tracking Capabilities \n(a) The Supplier must identify and document all Solution capabilities that collect, capture, record or \nmonitor information, including: \ni. \nsensors; \nii. \ncameras; \niii. image capture or recording; \niv. audio recording; \nv. \nlocation tracking; and \nvi. other monitoring, recording or data collection functions. \n(b) The Supplier must identify the purpose and use of any such capability and the type of information \nthat may be collected or generated. \n7.9.8 \nAdditional Requirements for Autonomous, Robotic, OT and IoT Solutions \n(a) Where the Solution incorporates autonomous, robotic, Operational Technology (OT) or Internet of \nThings (IoT) capabilities, the Solution must: \ni. \nimplement controls to prevent unauthorised access, manipulation, modification or compromise of \nthe device; \nii. \noperate in a safe and predictable state during loss of network connectivity; \niii. operate in a safe and predictable state during cloud service disruption; \niv. operate in a safe and predictable state during cyber security incidents affecting connected \nsystems; \nv. \ninclude safety mechanisms designed to protect patients, visitors, staff and healthcare operations \nin the event of malfunction, system failure or cyber security compromise; and \n\n \n \nPage | 11  \nOFFICIAL: Sensitive ? NSW Government \nOFFICIAL: Sensitive ? NSW Government \nvi. identify and manage dependencies on external networks, cloud services and third-party providers \nrequired for normal operation. \n(b) The Solution must be designed so that a cyber security event or loss of connectivity does not result in \nan unsafe operating condition for patients, visitors, staff or healthcare operations. \n7.9.9 \nAssurance and Assessment Requirements \n(a) The Supplier must, when requested by NSW Health, HealthShare NSW, eHealth NSW or their \nrepresentatives, participate in cyber security, privacy, architecture, technical and governance \nassurance activities. \n(b) Assurance activities may include, but are not limited to: \ni. \nSupplier Security Assessment Questionnaire (SSAQ) assessments; \nii. \narchitecture and technical assurance reviews; \niii. privacy and information governance assessments; \niv. network and infrastructure assessments; and \n(c) NSW Health and eHealth NSW assurance and deployment processes are required for \nimplementation within a healthcare environment. \n(d) The Supplier must provide reasonable information, documentation, and access necessary to support \nthese assurance activities. \n(e) Completion of applicable assurance activities may be required before the Solution progresses to a \ntrial, implementation, deployment or Contract award, as determined by NSW Health or HealthShare \nNSW. \n \n7.10 \nOperational Characteristics \n(a) Suppliers should declare key operational specifications, including: \ni. \nNoise levels during operation. \nii. \nBattery type, charging approach, and operational runtime. \niii. \nCharging or battery management strategy to support continuous operation. \niv. \nTank capacity, where applicable, and coverage per fill. \nNote: Information on dirt detection sensors may be provided where available but are not mandatory. \n7.11 \nCommercial and Lifecycle Information \n(a) Suppliers should provide indicative commercial information, including: \ni. \nCapital purchase cost for equipment. \nii. \nLeasing or as-a-service options, including commercial structure. \niii. \nBattery replacement costs and lifecycle. \niv. \nConsumable costs, including brushes, filters, and cleaning agents. \nv. \nExpected asset life and depreciation considerations. \nvi. \nEnd-of-life disposal or recycling arrangements. \nvii. \nWarranty terms and conditions. \nviii. \nAny implementation or onboarding costs. \nNote: HSNSW intends to undertake a proof-of-concept trial as phase 2 to this procurement to assess the \noperational suitability of proposed solutions within a live healthcare environment. Equipment selected for \n\n \n \nPage | 12  \nOFFICIAL: Sensitive ? NSW Government \nOFFICIAL: Sensitive ? NSW Government \nparticipation in the trial may be subject to direct purchase for the purposes of evaluation and deployment \nacross multiple sites during this period. \nSuppliers should ensure that pricing provided for capital purchase is clear, complete, and reflective of all \ncomponents required for operational use. \nIn addition, suppliers are requested to provide detailed information on alternative commercial models, \nincluding leasing, rental, or as-a-service arrangements. This information will inform potential future \nprocurement approaches beyond the trial phase, including scalability, whole-of-life cost optimisation, and \nalignment with operational funding models. \nHSNSW is seeking flexibility in commercial structures to support both initial evaluation and longer-term \nadoption, subject to performance, value for money, and organisational requirements. \n \n8. \nEOI Instructions and Key Information \n8.1 \nAuthorised Contact Officer \nYou may contact the following person if you have any questions or require clarification on any topics \ncovered in this EOI: \nAuthorised Contact Officer \nName \nMurray Affleck \nPhone No. \n02 8644 2528 \nEmail \nMurray.Affleck@health.nsw.gov.au \n8.2 \nClosing Date and Time \nAll responses to this EOI must be submitted via the NSW Government buy NSW website (detailed below) \nby the nominated closing date and time. \nLodgement Details \nWebsite \nhttps://buy.nsw.gov.au/opportunity \nClosing Date \n25 September 2026 \nClosing Time \n3:00 pm (Australian Eastern Standard Time or Australian daylight-saving time, \nas applicable) \n8.3 \nDocument Standards \nElectronic submissions must meet the following document standards: \n? \nResponse Schedules: Microsoft Word (.doc or .docx) or PDF (.pdf) \n? \nSupporting Documents: Microsoft Word (.doc or .docx), PDF (.pdf), Microsoft Presentation (.ppt or \n.pptx) or Microsoft Excel (.xls or .xlsx) \n \n \n\n \n \nPage | 13  \nOFFICIAL: Sensitive ? NSW Government \nOFFICIAL: Sensitive ? NSW Government \n8.4 \nProposed Timeframe \nProvisional Timetable \nEOI issued \n9 September 2026 \nEnd period for questions or requests for information \n16 September 2026 \nEOI closes \n25 September 2026 \nEvaluation of submissions \n28 Sep ? 16 Oct 2026 \nICT and Digital Security assessment \nOct ? Nov 2026 \nTarget date for next stage ? Trial Start Date \nFebruary 2027 \nTarget Date for Phae 3 Request for Tender \n2 July 2027 \nThe above timeline is provisional only. The Agency may, at its discretion, amend the provisional EOI \nprogram. \n9. \nEvaluation Criteria \nSelection will follow the Evaluation process as set out Part A - Section 13.1. \nThe evaluation criteria that will be used to evaluate the Respondents are specified below: \n9.1 \nNon-Price Criteria \nNon-Price Criteria \n1. Solution Overview \nAssessment will consider: \n? Clarity and completeness of the proposed solution \n? Suitability of technology type for healthcare environments \n? Alignment of proposed models to requirements \n? Demonstrated understanding of intended use \n2. Functional Capability and Operational Performance \nAssessment will consider: \n? Ability to achieve healthcare cleaning standards across hospital floor surfaces \n? Suitability for operation in public and non-clinical healthcare environments \n? Effectiveness in delivering consistent cleaning outcomes across routine and ad hoc scenarios \n? Capability to respond to unplanned cleaning events, including spill response and urgent cleaning needs \n? Overall performance capability, including coverage, reliability, and operational effectiveness \n3. Autonomous Capability  \nAssessment will consider: \n? Navigation and mapping capability \n? Obstacle detection and avoidance \n? Reliability of autonomous operation \n\n \n \nPage | 14  \nOFFICIAL: Sensitive ? NSW Government \nOFFICIAL: Sensitive ? NSW Government \n? Ability to operate in low or no connectivity environments \n4. Reporting and Analytics \nAssessment will consider: \n? Quality and usability of reporting outputs \n? Availability of performance and usage data \n? Dashboard functionality and insights \n? Auditability and traceability of activities \n5. Usability and Workforce Integration  \nAssessment will consider: \n? Ease of use for operators \n? Training requirements and complexity \n? Integration with existing workforce and workflows \n? Change management considerations \n6. Reliability, Maintenance and Support \nAssessment will consider: \n? Expected uptime and reliability \n? Maintenance requirements and servicing model \n? Availability of local support in Australia \n? Spare parts availability and lifecycle support \n7. Safety \nAssessment will consider: \n? Safety features and controls \n? Compliance with Australian standards \n? Suitability for use around patients, visitors, and staff \n? Incident response and emergency functionality \n8. Infection Prevention and Control (IPC) \nAssessment will consider: \n? Cleanability of equipment \n? Compatibility with healthcare cleaning agents \n? Controls to prevent cross-contamination \n? Evidence of IPC validation or assessment \n9. Cyber Security and Data Management \nAssessment will consider: \n? Data ownership, custody, access and governance \n? Data hosting, processing and transmission locations, including cloud architecture \n? Solution architecture, integrations, data flows and external dependencies \n? Cyber security controls, certifications and independent assurance \n? Authentication, role-based access control and privileged access management \n? Logging, monitoring, audit and security event capabilities \n\n \n \nPage | 15  \nOFFICIAL: Sensitive ? NSW Government \nOFFICIAL: Sensitive ? NSW Government \n? Privacy and information protection controls, including the handling of images, audio, location and other \ncaptured information \n? Backup, recovery, disaster recovery and business continuity arrangements \n10. Operational Characteristics \nAssessment will consider: \n? Noise levels and suitability for hospital use \n? Battery performance and runtime \n? Charging requirements and efficiency \n? Tank capacity and operational efficiency \n11. Commercial and Lifecycle Information \nAssessment will consider: \n? Overall cost structure and value for money \n? Transparency of lifecycle costs \n? Flexibility of commercial models \n? Warranty and end-of-life considerations \n12. Proof of Concept Trial \nAssessment will consider: \n? Ease of deployment and relocation \n? Lead times for supply \n? Practicality of trial implementation \n? Supplier support during trial phase \n10. \nTerms and Conditions of Participation \n10.1 \nNot an Offer \nThis EOI is not an offer. It is a formal request for Respondents to submit a response demonstrating their \neligibility or capability to meet the organisation?s qualitative criteria for the supply of goods and/or \nservices, as detailed in this Part A document. Nothing in this EOI should be interpreted as creating a \nbinding contract (express or implied) between the Agency and any Respondents for the supply of goods \nand/or services. \n10.2 \nNon-Disclosure Agreement \nRespondents selected to participate in the RFT Phase 2 will be required to enter into a Non-Disclosure \nAgreement with the Agency to ensure that all confidential information shared during the RFT process is \nkept confidential. \n10.3 \nOrganisation Rights \nNotwithstanding anything else in this EOI, and without limiting its rights at law or otherwise, the Agency \nreserves the right, in its absolute discretion at any time, to: \n(a) vary or extend any time or date specified in this Invitation for all or any Respondents; or \n(b) terminate the participation of any Respondent or any other person in the EOI process. \n10.4 \nFormat and content \nRespondents must ensure that the Response uses the format and addresses the content requirements set \nout in the EOI Response documents. \n\n \n \nPage | 16  \nOFFICIAL: Sensitive ? NSW Government \nOFFICIAL: Sensitive ? NSW Government \nWord or page limits (where specified) should be observed. The Agency may, at its discretion, disregard any \nparts of the Response that exceed any specified word or page limits. \nResponses and other information provided in the EOI must be submitted in writing and in English. \n10.5 \nAmendments to this EOI \nThe Agency may, at its discretion, amend this EOI by giving appropriate written notice of an amendment. \nIf the Agency varies or supplements this EOI, it will issue an Addendum and Reference Material (if \nrequired) on the NSW Government buy NSW website https://buy.nsw.gov.au/suppliers. In each case, an \nAddendum or Reference Material becomes part of this EOI. \nIt is the obligation of the Respondent to check if any Addenda or Reference Material were issued prior to \nthe Closing Date and Time, even if a Response has already been submitted. \nRespondents must check the buy NSW website and download all Addenda and Reference Material. \n11. \nLegal entity \nResponses must be submitted by a legal entity or, if a joint tender, consortium or otherwise, by legal \nentities with the capacity to contract. The Agency will only enter into any proposed Contract with such \nlegal entity or entities. \nAny Response which is submitted by a combination of two or more organisations, as a joint venture, a \nconsortium or otherwise, must include in the Response: \n(a) information for each member of the group. \n(b) a clear statement of the nature of the association between the members of the group and the basis \nupon which they propose to contract with the Agency; and \n(c) a statement that each member of the group is jointly and severally liable for the performance of the \nother members of the group under the resultant contract or that one member of the group is fully \nliable for the performance of all members of the group. \nThe Agency may ask a Respondent to provide evidence of its legal status or capacity to contract. If \nResponses from entities propose to contract in their capacity as trustees, such evidence may include \ncopies of the relevant trust deeds. Any evidence requested is to be provided within three (3) working days \nof the request. \n12. \nDiscontinuance of EOI Process \nWithout limiting its other rights under this EOI or at law or otherwise, the Agency reserves the right to \ndiscontinue the EOI process at any point, without making a determination regarding acceptance or \nrejection of Responses. The Agency will advise Respondents to this effect. \n13. \nEthics \nRespondents are to comply with the NSW Health Code of Conduct  \nhttps://www1.health.nsw.gov.au/pds/ActivePDSDocuments/PD2015_049.pdf \nand the NSW Government?s Procurement Policy Framework \nhttps://buy.nsw.gov.au/policy-library/policies/procurement-policy-framework \n \n \n \n\n \n \nPage | 17  \nOFFICIAL: Sensitive ? NSW Government \nOFFICIAL: Sensitive ? NSW Government \n14. \nLodgement of Responses \n14.1 \nValidity Period \nThe Respondent?s submission is to be valid throughout the EOI evaluation process from the lodgement \nclosing date. \nRespondents are to note that its submission to this EOI is made at its own cost, and that the Agency whilst \nmaking the request in good faith, is not obliged to proceed with this project. \n14.2 \nElectronic Lodgement of Responses \nRespondents should not wait until the nominated closing time to lodge their responses as difficulties in \nuploading responses to the website may be encountered where many users attempt to upload responses \nat the same time or while attempting to upload large file sizes or volumes. \nA response lodged wholly or partly after the Closing time will be registered as a Late Response and may \nbe excluded from evaluation. The Agency may, at its discretion, allow the evaluation of a Late Response, if \nit judges that the lateness is due to circumstances outside the control of the Respondent. \nBy lodging an EOI, the Respondent agrees that the EOI remains valid and open for acceptance by the \nAgency for the Tender Validity Period.  \n14.3 \nCommunications \nAny requests for clarification in relation to this EOI or the EOI Process must be: \n? \ncommunicated to the Contact Officer in writing; and \n? \nsubmitted by the last date for questions set out in section 6.4 or asked during any briefing session. \nThe Agency is not obliged to respond to any question or request. \nIn all cases the Agency reserves the right to forward the question and details of the Agency's response to \nall Tenderers on a non-attributable basis. However, the Agency will give the Tenderer the opportunity to \nwithdraw the question where the Tenderer has requested that the question not be disclosed. \nApart from the processes identified in these Conditions, Respondents must not contact any \nRepresentative of the Agency, NSW Health or the NSW Government (including any members of \nparliament or their advisers) to discuss any aspect of the EOI or future RFP process, except as authorised \nin writing by the Agency. \nResponses and other information provided in the EOI must be submitted in writing and in English. \n14.4 \nLate Responses \nLate Responses may not be considered, except where the Agency is satisfied that the integrity and \ncompetitiveness of the EOI process has not been compromised. The Agency will not penalise any \nRespondent whose response is received late, if the delay is due solely to mishandling by the Agency. \n14.5 \nExtension of Closing Date and Time \nThe Agency may, at its discretion, extend the Closing Date and Time. \n15. \nEvaluation \n15.1 \nEvaluation Process \n(a) The Agency will evaluate Expressions of Interest (EOIs) based on the criteria detailed in section B of \nthis EOI document. This evaluation aims to determine the suitability of potential suppliers for the next \nstage of the procurement process. The information provided by Respondents in their EOI Responses \nwill be used to assess how well they meet each criterion. \n\n \n \nPage | 18  \nOFFICIAL: Sensitive ? NSW Government \nOFFICIAL: Sensitive ? NSW Government \n(b) Respondents are strongly advised to provide clear and comprehensive responses to all evaluation \ncriteria outlined in this EOI document. This will help ensure that their submissions are properly \nassessed. \n(c) The Agency may exercise discretion in evaluating EOIs and managing the EOI Process. This includes \nthe evaluation of any subjective criteria where applicable. By submitting an EOI, Respondents \nacknowledge that the Agency?s decision on their EOI and those of other Respondents will be final and \nbinding. \n(d) The Agency may consider alternative proposals submitted alongside a conforming EOI. Such \nalternatives will be assessed based on their merit and alignment with the overall objectives of the \nprocurement. \n15.2 \nAcceptance or Rejection of EOIs \nThe Agency is not obligated to accept any EOI received. The decision to accept or reject EOIs is at the \nAgency?s sole discretion. \nThe Agency reserves the right to: \n(a) Accept or reject any EOI based on its alignment with the evaluation criteria and the Agency?s specific \nneeds and requirements. \n(b) Reject EOIs that do not meet the required format or content, or that lack sufficient information to \nenable a thorough evaluation. \n(c) Reject any non-co
